 (185,185,'Unknown','JAMES','ADAMSON','Lieutenant',334,'Imperial Light Horse/Kimberley Regiment, S.A.Forces',393,'Unknown',0,'1944-10-23','Killed in action, by machine gun fire, in the vicinity of Fossa, Italy. The War Diary records his death at 15:40 on the 22 October','Son of John and Isabella Jenkins Adamson (nee Anderson), of 20 Roxburgh Road, Selection Park, Springs, Transvaal. Born in Springs. A surveyor in the Mining industry. He was unmarried.','Italy',39,'CASTIGLIONE SOUTH AFRICAN CEMETERY',483,'44.14397','11.16183 ','II, D, 8.',NULL,'J','127881V','26','',0,'LG Sup 19 July 1945 - "For gallant and distinguished services in Italy"',0,''),
 (186,186,'Unknown','WILLIAM DOUGLAS','ADAMSON','Ordinary Seaman',389,'South African Naval Forces',831,'H.M.S. Repulse',2345,'1941-12-10','Imperial Japanese Navy land based bombers found the battleship HMS Prince of Wales (35,000 tons) and battle-cruiser HMS Repulse (26,500 tons) in the South China Sea. The British capitol ships are sunk, by 88 Japanese aircraft in less than two hours; 840 men die, 2,800 are rescued by escorting destroyers. This leaves the Allies with no active battleships in the Pacific.','Son of Mr. and Mrs. J. Adamson, of Klerksdorp, Transvaal, South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','Panel 58, Column 3.',NULL,'W D','69001','','Devon',2,'',0,''),
