 (198,198,'Unknown','HAROLD MELBOURNE','ADDISON','Sapper',469,'South African Engineer Corps',797,'31 Road Construction Coy.',1007,'1942-06-04','Accidentally injured (multiple burns) on 25 May 1942 and died in hospital on 4 June.','Son of Mr. E. Addison, of Salt River.','Egypt',23,'TEL EL KEBIR WAR MEMORIAL CEMETERY',2479,'30.56146','31.78723 ','3. C. 11.',NULL,'H M','172667','','',0,'',0,''),
 (199,199,'Unknown','NOEL GOODRICKE','ADDISON','Lieutenant',334,'1st King Edward&#39;s Horse',47,'Unknown',0,'1918-04-09','Killed in action, in defence of the area of Huit Maisons and Vieille Chapelle on 9-11 April 1918, facing the German onslaught in the opening phase of the Battle of the Lys.','Son of W. H. and Florence Addison, of Natal, South Africa. Educated at New College, Oxford. Educated at Hilton College, Natal','France',27,'LOOS MEMORIAL',1517,'50.461','2.77173 ','Panel 2.',NULL,'N G','','25','Pas de Calais',1,'LG Sup 2 December 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty during the defence of a village. He organised local counter-attacks when the enemy had penetrated into the position. It was largely due to his fine behaviour and leadership that these defences were held for three hours after the first penetration by the enemy."',0,''),
