 (809,809,'Unknown','PHILIP HENRY','AYDON','Master',346,'Merchant Navy',474,'S.S. Tinhow (Glasgow)',3015,'1943-05-11','At 04.08 hours on 11 May, 1943, the unescorted Tinhow (Master Philip Henry Aydon) was torpedoed and sunk by U-181 about 25 miles 230 degrees from Limpoo Light in the Mozambique Channel. The master, 24 crew members and 50 passengers were lost. 41 crew members, twelve gunners and 75 passengers were picked up by Portuguese fishing boats and landed at Lourcenco Marques on 13 May.','Son of the late Arthur Horatio and Benedicta Aydon. Husband of Margaret Agnes Aydon, of "Park Cliffe", 422 Currie Road, Durban, South Africa. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1939-1945','United Kingdom',90,'TOWER HILL MEMORIAL',2527,'51.50955','-0.07769 ','Panel 108.',NULL,'P H','','50','London',1,'',0,''),
 (810,810,'Unknown','LESLIE ROYSTON','AYLES','Warrant Officer Class II',595,'South African Armoured Corps',779,'6 Armoured Car Regiment',1404,'1942-11-18','Died of lung abscess following operation for perforated duodenal ulcer','Son of Charles and Margaret F. Ayles, of Durban, Natal, South Africa. ***Might be South African Tank Corps, 6th Armoured Car Regt.','Egypt',23,'HELIOPOLIS WAR CEMETERY',1114,'30.07973','31.32963 ','3. A. 14.',NULL,'L R','604','28','',0,'',334,'attd. 2nd Royal Natal Carbineers, S.A. Forces'),
 (811,811,'Unknown','FRANK WILLIAM','AYLETT','Warrant Officer Class II',595,'South African Air Force',775,'26 Sqdn.',836,'1944-10-03','Killed in action, Vickers Wellington Mk XI #HZ522, missing on patrol. Scrambled to go to the assistance of a Sunderland flying boat that had sent out a distress signal. No one knows what happened but towards evening another Sunderland spotted burning wreckage on the surface of the sea far away from where the crews of the originally missing Sunderland were eventually picked up by the hospital ship "Chantilly". The Wellington and its crew were officially posted "missing". After a few hours the posting was reclassified to "presumed dead". Subsequent searches found no trace of the missing Wellington or its 6 crew','Son of Bert Aylett and Gladys Dorothy Rowley, of Durban. Born Durban. An apprentice printer','Malta',51,'MALTA MEMORIAL',1588,'35.8947','14.50783 ','Panel 17, Column 2.',NULL,'F W','542987V','22','',0,'',0,''),
 (812,812,'Unknown','FRED BUTLER','AYLIFF','Driver',209,'Australian Field Artillery',199,'1st Bde.',527,'1915-04-06','Accidental death, railway accident at Alexandria','Son of Major W. E. Ayliff, late of the Cape Mounted Police. Brother of Ethel Todd, of Prieska, South Africa. Born Mount Frere, Cape Colony, South Africa. Attended St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown, South Africa. Formerly of Cape Mounted Police. Commemoration stone in Northern Cape, PRIESKA, Old cemetery. His brother also died on service','Egypt',23,'CAIRO WAR MEMORIAL CEMETERY',420,'30.01438','31.23075 ','B. 200.',NULL,'F B','505','29','',0,'',0,''),
