 (1891,1891,'Unknown','CECIL WILLIAM','BLAIN','Lieutenant',334,'Royal Air Force',635,'Unknown',0,'1919-01-22','Died in air crash, was flying Sopwith Camel C1588 at Martlesham Heath (an aircaft testing and experimental centre) when its port wing cracked in level flight at 450 feet and he side-slipped in and was killed. This Camel had been at Martlesham since May 1918 where it was used for propeller testing, and lubricant and engine testing, amongst other things. The aircraft had clocked only 8 hours flying time when it was lost.','Son of Arthur C. and Kathleen Blain. Born 7 October 1896, at Hooton, Cheshire. With his Observer (2nd Lt C D Griffiths) plus Capt W D S Sanday plus Lt C W Busk and Lt Mase claimed two Albatros two-seaters forced to land on 6 August 1916. With Griffiths, shot down and taken prisoner 7 August 1916 in Strutter A380. A successful POW escapee. On Stutterheim War Memorial, Eastern Cape','United Kingdom',90,'IPSWICH OLD CEMETERY',1201,'52.06697','1.17013 ','BA. I. 79.',NULL,'C W','','22','Suffolk',1,'LG Sup 3 June 1919 pg. 7033 - "In recognition of distinguished services rendered during the war"',0,''),
 (1892,1892,'Unknown','BENJAMIN CHARLES ROSS','BLAINE','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1916-10-12','Killed in action. On the 12th October 1916, the 2nd and 4th S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The assault died away because of machine guns nests, placed well back in prepared positions, which caught the attacking waves at long range.','Son of Ben Blaine, M.B., and Ethel Constance Blaine, of Alexandra Rd., King William&#39;s Town, Cape Province. Fought in Delville Wood in July 1916.','France',27,'WARLENCOURT BRITISH CEMETERY',2680,'50.08079','2.79911 ','VI. E. 5.',NULL,'B C R','5634','20','Pas de Calais',19,'',19,'"D" Coy.'),
 (1893,1893,'Unknown','ANDREW EDWARD','BLAIR','Lance Corporal',311,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1917-04-12','Killed in action, in the improvised and disastrous attack of the 9th (Scottish) Division on the German positions in the East of Fampoux. The 2nd SAI, who went in 400 strong, lost 16 officers and 285 men.','Son of Robert and Agnes Blair, of 43a, Brand St., Kroonstad, Orange Free State. Born at Fouriesburg, Orange Free State. Proceeding to Scotland, he entered George Watson&#39;s College in 1906. He won the Shooting Championship 1912, and returned to South Africa that year, and entered the School of Mines and Technology, Johannesburg, intending to qualify as a mining engineer. In 1914 he was mobilised with the Rand Rifles. He was released and returned to the School of Mines. In Jan. 1916 he enlisted for service.','France',27,'BROWN&#39;S COPSE CEMETERY, ROEUX',371,'50.30288','2.88379 ','II. D. 32.',NULL,'A E','8929','23','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
