 (1922,1922,'Unknown','CECIL FRANCIS RAMSDEN','BLAND','Lieutenant',334,'Royal Berkshire Regiment',649,'3rd Bn.',1097,'1919-07-07','Murdered by Bolsheviks','Son of John and Adelaide Sophie Ramsden Bland Bland. Born at Durban, Natal. Alternative Commemoration - buried in Semenovka (Bereznik) Cemetery Extension.','Russia',67,'ARCHANGEL ALLIED CEMETERY',82,'64.55279','40.54284 ','Sp. Mem. B9.',NULL,'C F R','','21','',0,'MC LG Sup 25 April 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. He led his platoon in an advance over most difficult ground, and after reaching his objective made a personal reconnaissance and obtained valuable information. He was also largely responsible for the successful evacuation of casualties under heavy fire over difficult ground." Croix de Guerre LG Sup 21 July 1919',0,''),
 (1923,1923,'Unknown','JOSEPH ARTHUR','BLAND','Sergeant',492,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'9 Sqdn.',1681,'1943-04-23','Killed in action, Lancaster III ED799, left Waddington 22 April 1943 and set course for the Furze area (Bay of Biscay) for a minelaying operation','Son of Benjamin Adam and Mary Campbell Bland; husband of Ruth Irene Bland. Of Southern Rhodesia. Entered Gaul House in 1929, having previously been at the Plumtree Prep. School since January 1927. After passing his Junior Certificate examination, he left Plumtree in 1933. Joe was keen on all sport. He played for the School 1st Cricket XI, 2nd XV Rugger team and represented the School at the inter-school sports in 1930. He attested in February 1942, and after receiving preliminary training in Rhodesia, proceeded overseas in June of the same year. Before the war he was a reduction officer on the Acturus Mine. On Plumtree School Roll of Honour.','United Kingdom',90,'RUNNYMEDE MEMORIAL',2216,'51.43804','-0.56503 ','Panel 142.',NULL,'J A','778988','26','Surrey',1,'',0,''),
