 (2653,2653,'Unknown','WALTER EDMISTON','BROADBENT','Corporal',166,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1917-04-12','Killed in action, in the improvised and disastrous attack of the 9th (Scottish) Division on the German positions in the East of Fampoux. The 2nd SAI, who went in 400 strong, lost 16 officers and 285 men.','Son of Mr. Richard Malthly and Mrs. J. A. Broadbent, of Ardmay, Garwick, Isle of Man.','France',27,'BROWN&#39;S COPSE CEMETERY, ROEUX',371,'50.30288','2.88379 ','III. D. 25.',NULL,'W E','3263','27','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
 (2654,2654,'Unknown','ROBERT GEORGE','BROADWOOD','Lieutenant General',341,'General Staff',342,'57th Division',1360,'1917-06-21','Died of wounds','Son of Thomas and Mary Alethia Broadwood. 12th Lancers, Gentleman Cadet Robert George Broadwood, from the Royal Military College, to be Second Lieuteuant, vice H. S. Richardson, promoted. Dated 22nd January, 1881; 12th Lancers, Captain Robert G. Broadwood in seconded for service on the Staff. Dated 3rd May, 1892; 12th Lancers, Brevet Colonel R. G. Broadwood, C.B., A.D.C. to the King (extra), removed from the regiment on the appointment to the Staff. Dated 9th December, 1902; Major-General Robert George Broadwood, C.B., General Officer Commanding in South China, to be Colonel 7 May 1909; Major-General Robert G. Broadwood, C.B., to be Lieutenant-General, vice Sir F. W. Kitchener, K.C.B., deceased. Dated 7th March, 1912.','France',27,'ANZAC CEMETERY, SAILLY-SUR-LA-LYS',77,'50.65271','2.76311 ','II. I. 6.',NULL,'R G','','55','Pas de Calais',1,'Osmanieh (Fourth Class) LG 7 Oct 1898',0,''),
