 (2670,2670,'Unknown','HUGH','BROGAN','Corporal',166,'Royal Scots Fusiliers',694,'1st Bn.',528,'1917-05-13','Died of wounds','Son of the late Mrs. Cathrine Brogan. Born at Old Cumnock, Scotland, 20/02/1887. Attested Transvaal Police 31/03/1913. Stationed Boksburg. Army Reservist. Released to rejoin Royal Scots Fusiliers 17/08/1914, no. 8571, rank Corp. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','France',27,'FAUBOURG D&#39;AMIENS CEMETERY, ARRAS',839,'50.28697','2.76065 ','V. E. 15.',NULL,'H','8571','30','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
 (2671,2671,'Unknown','FRANCIS WESLEY','BROKENSHA','Sergeant',492,'South African Corps of Signals',795,'Unknown',0,'1942-11-28','Lost at sea on S. S. Nova Scotia, torpedoed and sunk by U-177. En route from Aden to Durban, South Africa. Carrying 6 passengers, 11 military and naval personnel, 134 South African guards and 765 Italian civil internees','Son of Thomas and Sarah Brokensha; husband of May C. Brokensha, of Pietermaritzburg, Natal, South Africa. Awarded the Royal Humane Society&#39;s Medal. Commemoration stone in Dundee Cemetery','Egypt',23,'ALAMEIN MEMORIAL',35,'30.83823','28.94696 ','Column 141.',NULL,'F W','99156','47','',0,'From RHS - Brokensha, W. Case 38660 - At 11 a.m. on the 21st July, 1911, J.C. Burton was bathing in the sea at Amanzimtoti, Natal, and got into difficulty. W. Brokensha went to his help but could not save him, and was himself washed ashore half an hour after.',0,''),
