 (4508,4508,'Unknown','FREDERICK CARRUTHERS','CORNELL','Lieutenant',334,'South African Native Labour Corps',830,'Unknown',0,'1921-03-05','Died in motor cycle accident','Husband of Mrs. F. Cornell. Born in England, and educated at the Bedford Grammar School. He studied music and languages in London. In 1902, he came to South Africa, and displayed great interest in Namaqualand. He was an editor of "The Cape Register". His publications include a volume of reminiscences The Glamour of Prospecting (1920). His collection of short stories, some of which are considered to be among South Africa&#39;s finest, are found in A Rip Van Winkle of the Kalahari and Other Tales of South West Africa 1915. He also wrote martial poetry, including "A Soldier&#39;s Song" about Delville Wood in World War One. He was awarded the Order of the British Empire for his literary efforts. OBE LG Sup 4 Oct 1918 pg 11774','United Kingdom',90,'UNKNOWN CEMETERY',2572,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,'F C','','53','Unknown',1,'***Not yet accepted for War Grave Status by CWGC, need service file',0,''),
