 (4692,4692,'Unknown','EUSTACE ALVANLEY','CROLL-JONES','Second Lieutenant',475,'Royal Field Artillery',660,'38th Army Bde.',1078,'1918-04-15','Killed in action, at Neuve Eclise','Son of the late Hon. Mr. Justice Sydney Twentyman Jones and of Mrs. F. H. Jones, of Baroda, Cape Province, South Africa. Born 7/6/1891. Educated at St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown. At St Andrew&#39;s from Jan. 1902-Aug. 1905. Joined Imperial Light Horse when war broke out. Served in the Rebellion and G.S.W.A. Went to England, joined a cadet school and was commissioned to the Royal Field Artillery. Served in France through heavy fighting in 1917 and was killed in action in April 1918 during the great German offensive. Commemoration stone in Cape Town (MAITLAND) Cemetery. His brother S D S Jones also died on service','Belgium',10,'TYNE COT MEMORIAL',2552,'50.88745','3.00051 ','Panel 4 to 6 and 162.',NULL,'E A','','26','Zonnebeke, West-Vlaanderen',1,'',0,''),
 (4693,4693,'Unknown','CHARLES ROBERT COOPER','CROMBIE','Fourth Engineer Officer',264,'Merchant Navy',474,'S.S. Gretavale (Glasgow)',2941,'1941-11-02','Killed in action, U-202 attacked convoy SC-52 northeast of Notre Dame Bay, Newfoundland and observed hits on two ships and heard a third. At 08.44 hours, a capsized wreck was sunk by coup de grace, possibly that of one of the vessels struck during the first attack. However, the Flynderborg and Gretavale were sunk by these attacks. The master, 31 crew members and six gunners from the Gretavale (Master Frank S. Passmore) were lost. Six crew members were picked up by the HMCS Windflower (K 155) (Lt John Price) and landed at St.Johns.','Son of Walter and Edith Crombie; husband of Sarah M. Crombie, of Plean, Stirlingshire. Born 1912 in Cape Town. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1939-45','United Kingdom',90,'TOWER HILL MEMORIAL',2527,'51.50955','-0.07769 ','Panel 54.',NULL,'C R C','','29','London',1,'',0,''),
 (4694,4694,'Unknown','FRED','CROMPTON','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'3rd Regt.',1119,'1917-04-09','Killed in action, during first day of the Battle of Arras. The 3rd S.A.I. was on the right of the South African Infantry Brigade in the attack of Black Line and Blue Line (North of St Laurent-Blangy). The 3rd S.A.I. sustained 53 killed and 226 wounded','Son of Robert and Ann Crompton, of 3, Peel House Lane, Widnes, England.','France',27,'ROCLINCOURT VALLEY CEMETERY',2179,'50.33322','2.80449 ','IV. G. 17.',NULL,'F','833','30','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
