 (4840,4840,'Unknown','RODNEY ARCHIBALD','CURRIN','Captain',115,'South African Armoured Corps',779,'Unknown',0,'1944-09-16','Killed in action','Son of Mr. Archibald George Berry Currin and Mrs. Irene Maud Currin, of Port Elizabeth, Cape Province, South Africa.','Italy',39,'FAENZA WAR CEMETERY',828,'44.27573','11.89202 ','VII. F. 10.',NULL,'R A','8023V','28','',0,'',571,'seconded to 1st Bn. Hertfordshire Regt. Bedfordshire and Hertfordshire Regiment'),
 (4841,4841,'Unknown','RICHARD WILLIAM','CURRIN','Lieutenant Colonel',339,'South African Coast Defence Corps',790,'Unknown',0,'1942-06-12','Died of carcinoma of stomach','Born Cape Province. A retired commericial traveler. Non War dead in CWGC care','South Africa',72,'EAST LONDON (EAST BANK) CEMETERY',746,'-33.00852','27.91598 ','Sec.11C. Grave 743E.',NULL,'R W','','71','Eastern Cape',1,'DSO LG Sup 25 August 1916 pg. 8454 - "For conspicuous courage and ability in organising the defence of front line trenches, and by personla exampe and total disregard of danger securing good work under heavy fire. He was instrumental in bringing in eighty wounded from exposed positions"; 1st bar LG Sup 4 October 1919 pg. 12215 - "For conspicuous gallantry and able leadership in command of his battalion in the attack on Regnicourt on 17 October 1918. During the advance he organised and carried out an attack on a strongly defended locality, which had been passed by the leading waves of the attack on account of thick fog, and from which machine guns were firing into the backs of our advancing troops. By his prompt action the locality was captured, with upwards of 120 prisoners and twenty machine guns."',0,''),
 (4842,4842,'Unknown','CHARLES WILLIAM','CURTIS','Cyclist',171,'Cape Cycle Corps',253,'Unknown',0,'1918-10-17','Died on active service','Son of Mr. C. H. Curtis, of Church Street, Wynberg','South Africa',72,'CAPE TOWN (MAITLAND) CEMETERY',452,'-33.91592','18.52805 ','',NULL,'C W','B/4783','21','Western Cape',1,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',0,''),
 (4843,4843,'Unknown','EDWARD JOHN','CURTIS','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1917-04-12','Killed in action, received in the improvised and disastrous attack of the 9th (Scottish) Division on the German positions in the East of Fampoux. The 1st SAI lost 2 officers and 203 men.','Son of John and Elizabeth Curtis, of 6, York St., Grahamstown, Cape Province, South Africa. His brother also died on service. Commemoration stone in Grahamstown Old Catholic Cemetery.','France',27,'ATHIES COMMUNAL CEMETERY EXTENSION',111,'50.30399','2.83734 ','A. 1.',NULL,'E J','9739','37','Pas de Calais',10,'',10,'"B" Coy.'),
 (4844,4844,'Unknown','JAMES VINCENT','CURTIS','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Missing in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of John and Elizabeth Curtis, of 6, York St., Grahamstown, Cape Province, South Africa. Commemoration stone in Grahamstown Old Catholic Cemetery. His brother also died on service.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'J V','9309','20','Somme',10,'',10,'"B" Coy.'),
