 (6125,6125,'Unknown','CORNELIOUS JOHANNES','DU PREEZ','Sergeant',492,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'7 Sqdn.',1530,'1942-09-07','Kiled in action, Stirling W7629, on raid to Duisberg, crashed on the Baaler Bruch, 6 km, SSW of Goch','RAF Number: Enlistments in Rhodesia','Germany',29,'REICHSWALD FOREST WAR CEMETERY',2124,'51.74094','6.08179 ','Coll. grave 27. B. 1-6.',NULL,'C J','777663','','Kleve, Nordrhein-Westfalen',1,'LG Sup 15 May 1942 pg. 2122 - "One night in April, 1942, this airman was the rear gunner of an aircraft which attacked Hamburg. On the return journey his aircraft was engaged by an enemy fighter. Sergeant du Preez was seriously wounded in the neck, the wound being some 6 inches long and so deep that the jugular vein was exposed. Gallantly remaining at his post, this airman continued to use his guns, repelling subsequent attacks until he was rendered unconscious by a bullet which grazed his scalp. Throughout, Sergeant du Preez displayed great devotion to duty and, although so seriously injured, he contributed, materially to the safe return of his aircraft."',0,''),
 (6126,6126,'Unknown','CHARLES PATRICK HENEGAN','DU PREEZ','Able Seaman',25,'South African Naval Forces',831,'H.M.S. Cornwall',2279,'1942-04-05','Killed in action. CORNWALL and her sister ship Dorsetshire were detached from the Fleet when they were sighted by Japanese aircraft. A little later they were overwhelmed by approximately 50 dive-bombers whose accurate attack sank both ships quickly; 190 of Cornwall&#39;s crew of 650 were lost.','Son of John G. and Ellen E. du Preez, of Port Elizabeth, Cape Province, South Africa; husband of Lily du Preez, of Port Elizabeth. Commemoration stone in Port Elizabeth (South End) Cemetery','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','Panel 74, Column 2.',NULL,'C P H','68173','32','Devon',1,'',0,''),
 (6127,6127,'Unknown','CORNELIUS PETRUS HERCULAAS','DU PREEZ','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Killed in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Brother of Mrs. A. Pienaar, of 88, Perkin St., North End, Port Elizabeth, South Africa.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'C P H','825','34','Somme',10,'',10,'"B" Coy.'),
