 (7566,7566,'Unknown','MARINUS ANTON ALEXANDER','FRANKEN','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1916-10-12','Killed in action. On the 12th October 1916, the 2nd and 4th S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The assault died away because of machine guns nests, placed well back in prepared positions, which caught the attacking waves at long range.','Son of the late Marinus and Johanna Franken (nee Van Loon), of Pretoria.','France',27,'WARLENCOURT BRITISH CEMETERY',2680,'50.08079','2.79911 ','I. K. 13.',NULL,'M A A','8554','31','Pas de Calais',19,'',19,'"D" Coy.'),
 (7567,7567,'Unknown','GEORGE HILTON','FRANKIM','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'3rd Regt.',1119,'1917-07-19','Drowned, at Walton-on-Thames. Four men, convalescing at Walton Red Cross Hospital, Fink, Newberry, Frankim and Ferreira all went for a row in a single sculler, three sitting in the stern. It is presumed that the sculler wanted to change his position, and on getting up the bow of the boat tipped into the air and all were thrown into the water. Private Ferreira managed to reach the shore in an exhausted condition, but the others handicapped by their wounds and uniforms, sank before assistance could reach them.','Son of Henry John and Ellen Frankim, of Alice, Cape Province, South Africa. ***Possibly FRANKIN instead of FRANKIM','United Kingdom',90,'BROOKWOOD MILITARY CEMETERY',369,'51.30022','-0.64212 ','VII. F. 2.',NULL,'G H','9928','20','Surrey',1,'',0,''),
