 (8191,8191,'Unknown','JOHN','GILLESPIE','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'4th Regt.',1270,'1918-07-12','Killed in action, while serving his guns near Meteren, west of Bailleul','Husband of Mary Gillespie, of 60, Watson St., Aberdeen. Son of James Gillespie, cardmaker; born Stoneywood, Aberdeenshire, 17 February 1884; educated at Newhills School (where he became a pupil-teacher) and the Normal College ; matriculated in 1902 and graduated M.A., 1905. Teaching was his bent, and he proved himself a teacher of the best type, with high ideals of his profession. For some eight years he was second master at Cults Public School, then filled a post at the Training Institute, Morija, Basutoland, and later was Mathematical Master at the Boys&#39; High School, Rondebosch, Cape Town. Gillespie was a true pacifist, so sincere in his devotion to peace that he felt he must fight for her sake. In June 1916 he enlisted in the South African Scottish and came home to complete his training. After some months in England he crossed to France in April 1917, and saw some keen fighting with a Trench Mortar Battery in the 9th Division, of which the South Africans formed a part. His C.O. wrote : "For the short time he served in the Battery he made many friends, and his loss will be deeply felt by all, not only as a friend but also as an excellent soldier". He was killed in action while serving his guns near Meteren, west of Bailleul, 12 July 1918, having seen only three months of active service. Commemoration stone in Aberdeen (St. Peter&#39;s) Cemetery','France',27,'METEREN MILITARY CEMETERY',1659,'50.74349','2.68886 ','I. A. 51.',NULL,'J','16141','34','Nord',1,'',0,''),
 (8192,8192,'Unknown','PAUL ARCHIBALD','GILLESPIE','Major',343,'Royal Army Medical Corps',640,'Unknown',0,'1916-03-23','Died of bubonic plague','Son of the late Malcolm Gillespie. Husband of Johanna Sophia Elizabeth Gillespie. Born Sunderland; ed. Cannington Public School, Jarvis Street C.I., Toronto; Medicine 1887-91, M.B.; Practising at Penetanguishene and later at Winburg, S. Africa; Served in South African War; md. Jane Satchwell. In 1914 he joined the Royal Army Medical Corps, and served in the German West Africa campaign. When an outbreak of bubonic plague occurred he volunteered his assistance, and took charge till he contracted the disease himself and died at Winburg. Originally was caught up in diamond fever, but did serve twice in the military in South Africa. Family from Cannington, Ontario. One of three sons who were doctors, and part of a large family of a Scottish couple from the Isle of Islay, Argyleshire, Scotland.','South Africa',72,'WINBURG AREA CEMETERY ?',2733,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,'P A','','48','Free State',1,'***Not yet accepted for War Grave Status by CWGC, need service file',0,''),
