 (8285,8285,'Unknown','JOHN HENRY WELLINGTON','GODFREE','Flying Officer',259,'Royal Air Force',635,'Unknown',0,'1943-11-29','Killed in action, the Japanese decided to ship the sick back to Java a total of 640 men, including a number of Japanese sick patients, were taken on board the 4,645-ton passenger-cargo ship Suez Maru. In two holds, 422 sick British (including 221 RAF servicemen) and 127 sick Dutch prisoners, including up to twenty stretcher cases, were accommodated. The Japanese patients filled the other two holds. Escorted by a minesweeper W-12, the Suez Maru set sail from Port Amboina but while entering the Java Sea and about 327 kilometers east of Surabaya, Java, Netherlands East Indies, the vessel was torpedoed by the American submarine USS Bonefish commanded by Cdr. Tom Hogan. The ship started to list as water poured into the holds drowning hundreds, many managed to escape the holds and swam away from the sinking ship. The Japanese mine sweeper W-12 picked up the Japanese survivors, leaving between 200 and 250 men in the sea. At 14.50, the minesweeper, W-12, under orders from Captain Kawano, opened fire, using a machine gun and rifles. Rafts and lifeboats were then rammed and sunk by the W-12. The firing did not cease till all the prisoners were killed, the minesweeper then picked up speed and sped off towards Batavia (Jakarta) at 16.30 hours','Husband of Irene Mary Godfree, of Pietermaritzburg, Natal, South Africa.','Singapore',70,'SINGAPORE MEMORIAL',2302,'1.41847','103.75813 ','Column 423.',NULL,'J H W','117803','48','',0,'',0,''),
