 (9493,9493,'Unknown','HARRY PYNOR','HAWTIN','Flight Sergeant',257,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'55 Sqdn.',1346,'1944-08-25','Killed in action, Baltimore V FW390, failed to return from night intruder mission to Bologna, Italy','Son of Maj. Henry Hawtin, M.B.E., and Gladys Victoria Hawtin, of Sheffield. RAF number: Rhodesia for local enlistments','Italy',39,'CORIANO RIDGE WAR CEMETERY',577,'43.98464','12.60415 ','III, F, 5.',NULL,'H P','711033','20','',0,'',0,''),
 (9494,9494,'Unknown','JOHN JAMES ALEXANDER','HAWTREY','Second Lieutenant',475,'Royal Flying Corps',661,'60th Sqdn.',1437,'1917-09-17','Died of wounds, received on 16 September 1917, left at 1810 hours on an offensive patrol for Tenbrielen and was shot up and forced to land at 1830 hours. 2nd Lt. J.J.A. Hawtrey was missing in action and was taken prisoner of war and died of wounds on 17 September 1917. Was flying SE5 A8909','Next of kin, A. J. Hawtrey, of Ellerslie Road, Wynberg, Cape Province. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','Belgium',10,'HARLEBEKE NEW BRITISH CEMETERY',1077,'50.85942','3.32437 ','XIII. C. 9.',NULL,'J J A','','','Harelbeke, West-Vlaanderen',1,'',0,''),
 (9495,9495,'Unknown','ALASTAIR JAMES','HAY','Flight Lieutenant',253,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'182 Sqdn.',474,'1944-08-18','Killed in action, Battle of the Falaise Gap, Typhoon JP427, encountered flak near Vimoutiers and was shot down','Son of Frederick John Gordon Hay and Catherine, nee Metherell; ed. Christian Brothers&#39; College, Pretoria. (Member of Boys Naval Brigade); General Botha Cadet Draft 1937-8. After leaving General Botha, Alistair James Hay joined the Union-Castle Company, in which he remained until 1940, when he enlisted with the RAF and in 1942 became Pilot Officer and subsequently Flight-Lieutenant. He was awarded the DFC for distinguished service in Russian Convoys. Of South Africa. Born Johannesburg.','France',27,'ST. DESIR WAR CEMETERY',2341,'49.13847','0.16305 ','V. D. 4.',NULL,'A J','67093','22','Calvados',1,'LG Sup 23 June 1942 pg. 2756 - "Pilot Officer Hay was pilot of the Hurricane on board a ship fitted with a catapult. On the approach of enemy aircraft he was catapulted off and immediately proceeded to attack and drive off a formation of six Heinkel 111&#39;s and 115&#39;s which were preparing to deliver a torpedo attack on the port bow of the convoy; not only did this prevent synchronisation with an attack which developed from the starboard bow, but he destroyed one Heinkel 111 and slightly damaged another. Pilot Officer Hay was himself wounded and he then baled out and was picked up by one of His Majesty&#39;s ships of the convoy escort. He showed great gallantry and his spirited attack was a great encouragement to all the convoy and escorts, and cannot but have been a great discomfort and surprise to the enemy."',0,''),
 (9496,9496,'Unknown','EDWARD JAMES ALLAN','HAY','Lieutenant',334,'41st Dogras',101,'2nd Bn.',900,'1918-09-05','Died of enteric fever, at Ghatingri, Punjab.','Son of James Mess Allan and Helen Hay, of Glen Downe, Pietermaritzburg, Natal, South Africa. Born Pietermaritzburg. On Maritzburg College cenotaph. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','India',33,'PALAMPUR CHURCHYARD',2914,NULL,NULL,'Face 12.',NULL,'E J A','','19','',0,'',0,''),
 (9497,9497,'Unknown','GEORGE','HAY','Flight Sergeant',257,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'101 Sqdn.',159,'1943-06-26','Killed in action, Lancaster LM318, took off from RAF Ludford Magna on the night of 25/26th June 1943 detailed to bomb Ludwigshafen. Nothing was heard from the aircraft after take off and it failed to return to base. The aircraft crashed at Ermen, 4 kms south south east of Ludwigshafen and all the crew members were killed','RAF Number: Enlistments in Rhodesia','Germany',29,'REICHSWALD FOREST WAR CEMETERY',2124,'51.74094','6.08179 ','3. E. 15.',NULL,'G','778764','','Kleve, Nordrhein-Westfalen',1,'',0,''),
