 (10382,10382,'Unknown','JOSEPH LEIGH','HOWELL','Lance Sergeant',313,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-07-16','Killed in action, at Delville Wood. Brigadier-General Lukin was ordered to support the attack of the 11th Royal Scots [27th Brigade] on the orchard, situated in the northern sector of Longueval, between North Street and Flers Road. The Scots would attack along North Street while B and C Companies of the 1st SAI would attack northwards in the wood, parallel to the Royal Scots. The South African attack also failed. The enemy fire scythed down the advancing troops.','Son of Gilbert and Emma Mary Howell.','France',27,'DELVILLE WOOD CEMETERY, LONGUEVAL',645,'50.02438','2.8127 ','XV. L. 4.',NULL,'J L','3983','','Somme',15,'',15,'"C" Coy.'),
 (10383,10383,'Unknown','LOUIS FREDERICK','HOWELL','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'4th Regt.',1270,'1918-09-12','Drowned at sea (from S.S. "Galway Castle"), as a result of an attack by an enemy submarine 160 miles S.W. and .5 S. of the Fastnet','Son of Mrs. C. Howell, of Fairview, Johannesburg. On Bezuidenhout Valley War Memorial. Commemoration stone in Cape Town (Maitland) Cemetery','United Kingdom',90,'HOLLYBROOK MEMORIAL, SOUTHAMPTON',1156,'50.93384','-1.43114 ','Panel 49.',NULL,'L F','X/316','','Hampshire',1,'',0,''),
