 (10574,10574,'Unknown','THOMAS EDWIN','HURLE','Gunner',278,'South African Heavy Artillery',804,'74th Siege Bty.',1576,'1916-11-20','Died of wounds. The Battery was at Colincamp since the 7th October 1916 in a position really untenable for a heavy battery, without cover and a favourite target for the German artillery. On the 20th November, the enemy kept up a severe bombardment all day, and four gunners were killed (the three men buried at Courcelles and Bombardier Frank Birt buried in Couin British Cemetery).','Son of George and Mary Hurle.','France',27,'COURCELLES-AU-BOIS COMMUNAL CEMETERY EXTENSION',585,'50.10582','2.58548 ','C. 13.',NULL,'T E','762','36','Somme',1,'',0,''),
