 (10940,10940,'Unknown','PAUL LEON LAMBERT','JACQUES-HOUSSA','Lieutenant',334,'South African Air Force',775,'60 Sqdn.',1426,'1943-07-27','Killed in action, 2 crew (Belgian) crashed, in De Havilland DH98 Mosquito #DD744','Son of Charles and Madeline Jacques-Houssa, of Belgium. Husband of Denise Jacques-Houssa, of Johannesburg. Born Leige, Belgium','Malta',51,'MALTA MEMORIAL',1588,'35.8947','14.50783 ','Panel 12, Column 1.',NULL,'P L L','205632V','29','',0,'',0,''),
 (10941,10941,'Unknown','ISAAC','JAFFE','Steward',537,'Merchant Navy',474,'S.S. Oropesa (Liverpool)',2989,'1941-01-16','Killed in action. On 16 January 1941 at 0356 hrs Oropesa was southeast of Rockall in the Western Approaches when the German Type VIIC submarine U-96, commanded by Kptlt Heinrich Lehmann-Willenbrock, hit her in the stern with one G7a torpedo. Oropesa did not sink immediately, so U-96 waited and at 0440 hrs fired another torpedo, but that malfunctioned and went in circles. U-96 fired again at 0503 and 0559 hrs, this time hitting Oropesa beneath her bridge and amidships. The liner capsized and sank at 0616 hrs','ref. South African Roll of Honour 1939-45','United Kingdom',90,'TOWER HILL MEMORIAL',2527,'51.50955','-0.07769 ','Panel 77.',NULL,'I','','22','London',1,'',0,''),
 (10942,10942,'Unknown','JOSEPH','JAFFE','Captain',115,'Royal Army Medical Corps',640,'Unknown',0,'1918-08-01','Killed in action','Only son of Mrs. Ida Jaffe, of Oudtshoorn, Cape Province, South Africa. Son of David Jaffe, storekeeper, Addo Station, Cape Colony; born Shavli, Russia, 6 July 1888; entered the University as a student in Medicine, summer session, 1908, and graduated M. B., 1913. He returned to South Africa and set up in general medical practice at Addo Station; this however he carried on for only a short time. Jaffe was one of those who came from the other end of the world to fight for King and Country. In May 1916 he obtained a commission in the R.A.M.C. and was promoted Captain in May 1917. He was attached as Regimental Medical Officer to the Somerset Light Infantry, went abroad in May 1916 and saw service in India, Egypt and France. ***Jewish','France',27,'RAPERIE BRITISH CEMETERY, VILLEMONTOIRE',2111,'49.29174','3.32559 ','IA. B. 2.',NULL,'J','','30','Aisne',342,'',342,'attd. 2nd/4th Bn. Somerset Light Infantry'),
