 (11175,11175,'Unknown','LEONARD THOMAS','JEREY','Sergeant',492,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'Unknown',0,'1941-02-17','Died in Aircraft Accident, in Harvard P5856','(Name may have been spelled Jearey). Born Muizenberg, Cape, in 1910, and entered Lloyd House in 1922. Before leaving in 1927 he was a lance-corporal in the Cadets; a member of the Rugby XV; represented the School boxing team from 1923 to 1927, winning in the latter year the inter-school light-heavy championship; in 1924 he won the School under 14 swimming championship. In 1931, he went to England and served in the Air Force there for three years with a short-service commission. He took a prominent part in boxing and represented the R.A.F. After serving with the R.A.F. in Egypt, he returned to Rhodesia and went mining. He joined the Air Force again soon after the outbreak of war. On Plumtree School Roll of Honour','Zimbabwe',95,'HARARE (PIONEER) CEMETERY',1061,'-17.85092','31.03899 ','Eur. War Graves Plot. Grave 21.',NULL,'L T','778264','31','Harare (Salisbury)',1,'',0,''),
 (11176,11176,'Unknown','FRANK HORACE','JERRED','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1916-10-12','Missing in action. On the 12th October 1916, the 2nd and 4th S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The assault died away because of machine guns nests, placed well back in prepared positions, which caught the attacking waves at long range.','Son of the late Samuel and Annie Jerred, of Exeter, England.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'F H','8931','29','Somme',19,'',19,'"D" Coy.'),
