 (11907,11907,'Unknown','ROBERT MICHAEL','KEATING','Lance Corporal',311,'South African Corps of Signals',795,'Unknown',0,'1940-06-30','Died of influenza and pneumonia, at Potchefstroom','Son of William T. Keating and Winifred S. Keating, of Johannesburg. Born Lethbridge, Canada. A radio mechanic.','South Africa',72,'JOHANNESBURG (BRIXTON) CEMETERY',1220,'-26.19806','28.01538 ','Gen. Sec. Grave 199/201G.',NULL,'R M','34597','22','Gauteng',1,'',0,''),
 (11908,11908,'Unknown','WILLIAM BRITTEN','KEATING','Captain',115,'Seaforth Highlanders',752,'9th Bn.',1720,'1918-10-11','Died of pneumonia','Son of Alfred and Louisa Emily (nee Charlton) Keating. Native of Sydenham, London. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','Belgium',10,'LIJSSENTHOEK MILITARY CEMETERY',1477,'50.82839','2.7016 ','XXX. B. 15.',NULL,'W B','','38','Poperinge, West-Vlaanderen',1,'LG Sup 18 July 1917 pg. 7234 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. On two separate occasions he carried out a most difficult reconnaissance under very heavy fire in advance of our front line in order to enable fresh trenches to be constructed. The task was successfully accomplished entirely owing to his skilful use of ground and fearless leadership in the construction of the work."',0,''),
