 (12063,12063,'Unknown','DESMOND THOMAS','KENYON','Captain',115,'Pretoria Regiment, S.A. Forces',566,'Unknown',0,'1944-07-02','Died of wounds, received in artillery barrage at the road junction, west of Sinalunga, Italy. Their advance was held up by a congestion of tanks and self-propelled guns and this offered a good target for enemy artillery and mortars. They were part of the PR reconnaissance troop','Son of George Inglis and Sylvia Anne Kenyon (nee Hitge), of "The Residency", Lady Frere, Cape Province. Born Umtata, Cape Province. A scholar. He was unmarried.','Italy',39,'FOIANO DELLA CHIANA WAR CEMETERY',869,'43.2619','11.82189 ','II. N. 5.',NULL,'D T','41258V','24','',0,'',0,''),
 (12064,12064,'Unknown','GRAEME ALEXANDER BRUCE','KENYON','Able Seaman',25,'South African Naval Forces',831,'H.M.S. Cornwall',2279,'1942-04-05','Killed in action. CORNWALL and her sister ship Dorsetshire were detached from the Fleet when they were sighted by Japanese aircraft. A little later they were overwhelmed by approximately 50 dive-bombers whose accurate attack sank both ships quickly; 190 of Cornwall&#39;s crew of 650 were lost.','Son of David and Grace A. Kenyon; husband of Margaret Kenyon, of Rondebosch, Cape Town, South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','Panel 74, Column 2.',NULL,'G A B','68002','35','Devon',2,'',0,''),
