 (12697,12697,'Unknown','ERNEST CECIL','KRUUSE','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1917-04-12','Killed in action, in the improvised and disastrous attack of the 9th (Scottish) Division on the German positions in the East of Fampoux. The 1st SAI lost 2 officers and 203 men.','Son of Peter Martin Kruuse and Ada Winifred Fletcher Kruuse, of The Post Office, East London, Cape Province. Commemoration stone in Eastern Cape, GONUBIE, Presbyterian Church, graveyard','France',27,'BROWN&#39;S COPSE CEMETERY, ROEUX',371,'50.30288','2.88379 ','III. A. 1.',NULL,'E C','9370','26','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
 (12698,12698,'Unknown','ISAAC SIMON','KRUYER','Mess Room Boy',353,'Merchant Navy',474,'M.T. Barbro (Norway)',2531,'1941-09-19','Sunk by U-552, on a voyage from Halifax to Liverpool when she was sunk with 2 torpedoes from U-552 (Topp) just before midnight on September 19-1941 while in Convoy SC 44, escorted by 1 destroyer and 4 corvettes, Honeysuckle and Mayflower being 2 of them. Barbro had a cargo of 9000 tons petrol and all on board her, 21 Norwegians, 3 Swedes, 1 Dane, 5 British and 4 Canadians died in the flames, helplessly watched by other ships around her. Sank in position 61 30N 35 07W 35 11','Son of Abraham and Beatrice Kruyer, of Cape Town, South Africa. Signed on 6th August 1941 in UK. 1 year at sea. ***Jewish.','United Kingdom',90,'TOWER HILL MEMORIAL',2527,'51.50955','-0.07769 ','Addenda Panel',NULL,'I S','','16','London',1,'',0,''),
