 (12861,12861,'Unknown','MICHAEL','LAMBERT','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1917-04-12','Killed in action, in the improvised and disastrous attack of the 9th (Scottish) Division on the German positions in the East of Fampoux. The 2nd SAI, who went in 400 strong, lost 16 officers and 285 men.','Son of Mr. T. S. and Mrs. Sarah Lambert, of "Bellevue" Main St., Kokstad, Cape Province. On Mount Currie National Park Boy Scout War Memorial near Kokstad','France',27,'BROWN&#39;S COPSE CEMETERY, ROEUX',371,'50.30288','2.88379 ','II. D. 43.',NULL,'M','10083','21','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
 (12862,12862,'Unknown','PETER DENNIS','LAMBERT','Sergeant',492,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'Unknown',0,'1941-12-07','Died in Aircraft Accident, Spitfire I R6969','Son of Kenneth Norman and Charlotte Kathleen Lambert, of Shabani, Southern Rhodesia. Entered Lloyd House in January 1935. He took a prominent part in swimming. After leaving in 1937 he went to the Bulawayo Technical School. He attested in the R.A.F. in January 1941, and proceeded to England in August. On Plumtree School Roll of Honour','United Kingdom',90,'LLANTWIT MAJOR CEMETERY',1499,'51.40668','-3.46981 ','Sec. C. Grave 19.',NULL,'P D','778556','20','Glamorganshire',1,'',0,''),
