 (13108,13108,'Unknown','MURRAY STEWART','LE MARE','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'3rd Regt.',1119,'1917-01-12','Killed in action','Son of E. E. Le Mare, of Johannesburg, Transvaal, and the late T. A. Britton. On Bezuidenhout Valley War Memorial. By a cruel stroke of fortune his death occurred 48 hours before he was due to proceed to England to train for a commission. Attended Jeppe High School for Boys. Le Mare entered the Preparatory, January 1909, and left after matriculation December, 1914. Was Captain of the School, in both elevens and first Chairman of the Debating Society. He was in the Native Affairs Department. On Jeppe High School for Boys Roll of Honour','France',27,'FAUBOURG D&#39;AMIENS CEMETERY, ARRAS',839,'50.28697','2.76065 ','III. B. 7.',NULL,'M S','3771','19','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
 (13109,13109,'Unknown','GERALD JOHN','LE MESURIER','Major',343,'South African Air Force',775,'1 Sqdn.',117,'1943-07-08','Died in Aircraft Accident, air crash, mid-air collision in Master III W8453 while practicing dive-bombing on the Hilmarton Bombing Range, with Flight Lieutenant Lewis','Son of the Revd. Canon Thomas G. le Mesurier and Annalie le Mesurier; husband of Phyllis D. M. le Mesurier, of Pretoria, Transvaal, South Africa. A South African Ace he had 3 Destroyed / 1 Probably Destroyed / 1 Damaged. Remembered on Diocesan College Roll of Honour. He attended Diocesan College 1922-32.','United Kingdom',90,'BROOKWOOD MILITARY CEMETERY',369,'51.30022','-0.64212 ','1. J. 3.',NULL,'G J','102636','29','Surrey',1,'LG 23 April 1943 pg. 1855 - "This officer completed much operational flying in the Western Desert, displaying fine leadership and great courage throughout. On one occasion when 14 Stukas were shot down without loss to the Squadron, Major le Mesurier, flew with great distinction. His skilful leadership contributed materially to the success achieved. This officer has destroyed one enemy aircraft and probably destroyed several others."',0,''),
