 (13323,13323,'Unknown','PATRICK','LENNON','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'3rd Regt.',1119,'1917-10-17','Killed in action','Foster-son of Julia McDonough, of The Green, Finglas, Co. Dublin, Ireland.','Belgium',10,'POELCAPELLE BRITISH CEMETERY',1991,'50.92103','2.97194 ','XXXVIII. F. 6.',NULL,'P','7629','20','Langemark-Poelkapelle, West-V.',1,'',0,''),
 (13324,13324,'Unknown','ALFRED EDWARD','LENNOX','Lance Sergeant',313,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Killed in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench','Son of the late Edward Dawson Lennox, of Mount View Terrace, St. Luke&#39;s, Cork, Ireland. Served with the Cape Mounted Police in the South African War (Queen&#39;s Medal with Clasp) and with the South African Mounted Rifles in German South West Africa, 1914-1915','France',27,'CABARET-ROUGE BRITISH CEMETERY, SOUCHEZ',417,'50.38061','2.7416 ','XXX. E. 21.',NULL,'A E','8471','40','Pas de Calais',15,'',15,'"C" Coy.'),
 (13325,13325,'Unknown','FREDERICK GEORGE','LENNOX','Private',429,'Natal Light Horse',501,'"A" Sqdn.',9,'1914-11-18','Died of wounds, received at "Kheis Drift"','Son of Thomas and Alice Lennox, of 30 Alexander Street, Old Kent Road, London, SE. An engineer of Dundee, Natal','South Africa',72,'WINSTEAD FARM CEMETERY, GROBLERSHOOP',2750,'-28.92172','22.1555 ','',NULL,'F G','94','','Northern Cape',1,'',0,''),
 (13326,13326,'Unknown','JAMES','LENNOX','Warrant Officer Class II',595,'South African Medical Corps',817,'Unknown',0,'1945-10-25','Died on active service','Husband of Minah Jacomina Jacoba Lennox, of Cape Town.','South Africa',72,'JOHANNESBURG (WEST PARK) CREMATION MEMORIAL',1225,'-26.16244','27.98672 ','',NULL,'J','48759','','Gauteng',1,'',0,''),
 (13327,13327,'Unknown','JOHN GREY','LENNOX','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1916-10-15','Died of wounds, received in front the Butte de Warlencourt. On the 12th October, the 2nd and 4th S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The assault died away because of machine guns nests, placed well back in prepared positions, which caught the attacking waves at long range. The two battalions, which had suffered heavily, were relieved by the 3rd S.A.I. during the night of the 12th-13th October.','Son of James and Emily Lennox.','France',27,'BAZENTIN-LE-PETIT MILITARY CEMETERY',171,'50.03267','2.76022 ','B. 10.',NULL,'J G','9347','36','Somme',6,'',6,'"A" Coy.'),
