 (14082,14082,'Unknown','PATRICK JOHN','LYNCH','Warrant Officer Class II',595,'Prince Alfred&#39;s Guard, S.A.Forces',567,'Unknown',0,'1943-02-01','Accidentally drowned, while attending a Leadership Course AFV TC course, outside of Cullinan','Son of John and Dora Lynch, of 141 Russell Road, Port Elizabeth. Husband of Mrs. J. J. Lynch, of 53 Pearson Street, Port Elizabeth. Born in Port Elizabeth. A clerk for the SAR&H','South Africa',72,'PORT ELIZABETH (SOUTH END) CEMETERY',2014,'-33.97419','25.62525 ','S Extension. R.C. Grave 88S.',NULL,'P J','10912','24','Eastern Cape',1,'',0,''),
 (14083,14083,'Unknown','THOMAS','LYNCH','Lieutenant',334,'Princess Patricia&#39;s Canadian Light Infantry, R.C.I.C.',569,'Unknown',0,'1942-09-29','Died of cancer, at Deer Lodge hospital','Cousin of Frank Eason, of Bredenbury, Saskatchewan. Born at Port St. John, South Africa, and served in the Royal Warwickshire Regiment of the British Army 1914-26.','Canada',14,'WINNIPEG ASSUMPTION GARDENS',2749,'49.8791','-97.33118 ','Plot 168. Sec. B. Grave 12.',NULL,'T','','44','Manitoba',1,'MC LG Sup 16 September 1918 pg. 10981 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty when in command of a successful raid. He captured a prisoner and demolished the enemy&#39;s trenches, afterwards withdrawing his party without loss. He had previously reconnoitred this position both by day and night". DCM LG Sup 22 September 1916 pg. 9296 - "For conspicuous gallantry and ability when in command of a platoon in action. He led patrols and obtained valuable information under heavy shell and sniping fire. On one occasion he carried back a dangerously wounded officer, and then assisted to dig out three men buried by shell explosion". MM LG Sup 14 December 1916 pg. 12221',0,''),
