 (14127,14127,'Unknown','ARTHUR','MAASDORP','Second Lieutenant',475,'South African Heavy Artillery',804,'73rd Siege Bty.',1569,'1918-04-18','Died of wounds','Son of Christian George and Ella Elizabeth Maasdorp, of Cape of Good Hope. On Rondebosch War Memorial in St. Paul&#39;s Anglican Churchyard. On Cape Town "Company Gardens" Artillery Memorial. His brother Norman Maasdorp also died in service. Commemoration stone in Cape Town (Mowbray) Garden of Remembrance','France',27,'PERNES BRITISH CEMETERY',1933,'50.48333','2.3958 ','I. B. 4.',NULL,'A','','21','Pas de Calais',1,'LG Sup 26 July 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty during a hostile attack in command of his section. For a long period the battery position was subjected to heavy shelling and machine-gun fire, causing severe casualties, but by his courage and resource and fine example he inspired his men, and kept his guns in action throughout the day, affording support to the infantry at a very critical time."',0,''),
 (14128,14128,'Unknown','CHARLES ROLAND','MAASDORP','Second Lieutenant',475,'Royal Flying Corps',661,'43rd Sqdn.',1202,'1918-03-28','Died of wounds, flying on Sopwith Camel, shot down by Lt Udet, Ja11, between Albert and Bapaume','Son of Mr. James William Maasdorp, of P.O. Cedarmont, Transvaal. Was studying law when war broke out','France',27,'ARRAS FLYING SERVICES MEMORIAL',95,'50.287','2.76039 ','',NULL,'C R','','23','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
 (14129,14129,'Unknown','NORMAN','MAASDORP','Captain',115,'Royal Field Artillery',660,'Unknown',0,'1916-08-28','Died of wounds, received in France','Son of the Honorable Christian George and Ella Elizabeth Maasdorp, of Hillside, Rondebosch, Cape Province, South Africa. His brother Arthur Maasdorp also died on service. On Rondebosch War Memorial in St. Paul&#39;s Anglican Churchyard. Commemoration stone in Cape Town (Mowbray) Garden of Remembrance. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918.','United Kingdom',90,'KENSAL GREEN (ALL SOULS&#39;) CEMETERY',1274,'51.52812','-0.21732 ','57. 4. 45236.',NULL,'N','','30','London',1,'LG Sup 1 January 1916 pg. 17',0,''),
 (14130,14130,'Unknown','','MAASDORP','Private',429,'Cape Corps, S.A. Forces',252,'Unknown',0,'1947-02-10','Died after discharge','Alternative Commemoration - buried in Upington (Kameelmond) Cemetery, Cape Province','South Africa',72,'UPINGTON (STATION) CEMETERY',2575,'-28.45339','21.23996 ','Military Plot (A/C. Mem.).',NULL,'W','C/288122','','Northern Cape',2,'',0,''),
 (14131,14131,'Unknown','REUBEN','MAASTRECHT','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Killed in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of John L. and Eliza Jane Maastrecht, of Carmel House, Mostert Rd., Observatory, Cape Town.','France',27,'WARLENCOURT BRITISH CEMETERY',2680,'50.08079','2.79911 ','I. E. 3.',NULL,'R','4120','21','Pas de Calais',10,'',10,'"B" Coy.'),
