 (14813,14813,'Unknown','HUGH FRANCIS PHILIP','MALLET','Sergeant',492,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1918-10-08','Killed in action, during the attack on Beaurevoir','Husband of Lillian Mallet, of 50, Cornelia St., Johannesburg. Educated at St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown','France',27,'BEAUREVOIR COMMUNAL CEMETERY BRITISH EXTENSION',187,'49.99245','3.31745 ','D. 15.',NULL,'H F P','5575','33','Aisne',1,'LG Sup 3 October 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty during three days&#39; fighting. When the line was under heavy fire he by good shooting and disregard of danger put a machine-gun out of action and kept the enemy fire down. He then organised and led an attack and captured his objective. When the enemy attacked next day, he set a fine example to his men by his coolness and devotion to duty under heavy artillery and machine-gun fire."',0,''),
