 (15414,15414,'Unknown','JOHN NORMAN','MASON','Lieutenant',334,'Dorset Yeomanry (Queen&#39;s Own)',286,'1st/1st',585,'1918-10-13','Died of wounds, received at El Reinte, on 27th September','Son of Elizabeth Whinneral Mason, of Ulverston, Lancs, and the late Thomas Walton Mason; husband of Effie Mason, of 11, Leinster Square, Rathmines, Dublin. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','Egypt',23,'ALEXANDRIA (HADRA) WAR MEMORIAL CEMETERY',49,'31.20378','29.92303 ','B. 89.',NULL,'J N','','32','',0,'LG Sup 7 November 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. When the squadron was suddenly fired on at daybreak he collected three men on the spur of the moment and charged a large body of the enemy with drawn swords, inflicting several casualties and putting them to flight. He then fought his way back, assisting one of his wounded men some way until the man died. Thanks to his courage and determination, his squadron leader had time to rally his squadron and consolidate his position."',0,''),
 (15415,15415,'Unknown','LANCELOT WILLIAM HART','MASON','Second Lieutenant',475,'West Yorkshire Regiment (Prince of Wales&#39;s Own)',966,'1st Bn.',528,'1917-04-14','Died on active service','Son of William Hart Mason and Elizabeth Agnes Mason, of Knysna, Cape Colony, South Africa. Educated at St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown. Born 25/2/1889, was at St Andrew&#39;s from 1907-1908, got rugby, cricket and athletics colours. Went to Europe after leaving school and joined SA Infantry when war broke out, was serving as 2nd Lieutenant in 1st West Yorks when he was killed','France',27,'PHILOSOPHE BRITISH CEMETERY, MAZINGARBE',1951,'50.47092','2.74168 ','I. N. 50.',NULL,'L W H','','28','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
