 (18252,18252,'Unknown','WILLIAM','MYERS','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Missing in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Brother of Mrs. Annie Curtayne, of 63 and 64 Fletcher&#39;s Chambers, Long Street, Cape Town','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'W','8563','26','Somme',1,'',0,''),
 (18253,18253,'Unknown','STANLEY SYDNEY','MYERSON','Sergeant',492,'Imperial Light Horse, S.A. Forces',392,'1st',515,'1942-08-15','Died of wounds, received while travelling on the main road at El Alamein. He was caught in a Stuka raid and died of shrapnel wounds','Son of Lazar and Mary Myerson, of 53 Braemer Court, Claim Street, Hillbrow, Johannesburg. Born in Johannesburg. A motor mechanic. He was unmarried. ***Jewish','Egypt',23,'EL ALAMEIN WAR CEMETERY',775,'30.83819','28.94707 ','XXIII. G. 23.',NULL,'S S','31284','24','',0,'',0,''),
