 (18603,18603,'Unknown','JOHN DANIEL','NEWBERRY','Flight Commander',252,'Royal Naval Air Service',680,'Chingford NAS',2032,'1917-09-28','Died of injuries, received in air crash in UK','When war broke out he was at Cambridge studying Engineering and joined the Royal Naval Air Service. Sent to Eastern France and conducted many raids from Belfort and won the French Croix de Guerre. Educated at St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown. At School from January 1911 to December 1912.','United Kingdom',90,'CHINGFORD MOUNT CEMETERY',526,'51.62094','-0.01617 ','D. 10. 5522.',NULL,'J D','','23','Essex',1,'LG Sup 23 August 1919 - "For valuable services rendered in connection with the war"',0,''),
 (18604,18604,'Unknown','WILLIAM','NEWBERRY','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1917-07-19','Drowned, at Walton-on-Thames. Four men, convalescing at Walton Red Cross Hospital, Fink, Newberry, Frankim and Ferreira all went for a row in a single sculler, three sitting in the stern. It is presumed that the sculler wanted to change his position, and on getting up the bow of the boat tipped into the air and all were thrown into the water. Private Ferreira managed to reach the shore in an exhausted condition, but the others handicapped by their wounds and uniforms, sank before assistance could reach them.','Son of James Arthur and Annie Jane Newberry. Born at Cape Town.','United Kingdom',90,'BROOKWOOD MILITARY CEMETERY',369,'51.30022','-0.64212 ','VII. F. I.',NULL,'W','7070','30','Surrey',1,'',0,''),
