 (19556,19556,'Unknown','GEOFFREY MICHAEL','PADDISON','Private',429,'Royal Natal Carbineers, S.A. Forces',679,'1st',515,'1941-12-02','Died of wounds, gun shot wound to stomach, received 9 miles east of Sidi Rezegh on 30 November. He was treated at 15 Field Ambulance, but died at Taiebelessem','Second son of Sir George Frederick Paddison, K.B.E., C.S.I., I.C.S., M.A.(Oxon), J.P., and Lady Eleanor Letitia Paddison, of Ingleby, 10 Grove Road, Merrow, Guildford, Surrey, England. Born at Roehampton, England. Educated at Rugby School, England, from 1928 to 1933. Corpus Christi College, Oxford University, England, 1933 to 1937; B.A. 1937; Grenoble University in France in 1937. Schoolmaster at Hilton College from 1938 to 1940.','Egypt',23,'HALFAYA SOLLUM WAR CEMETERY',1044,'31.54956','25.16047 ','B.A. 24. B. 6.',NULL,'G M','144802','27','',0,'',0,''),
 (19557,19557,'Unknown','ALEXANDER CHARLES','PADDOCK','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Missing in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of the late Charles Edward and Mary Alice Paddock.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'A C','6661','21','Somme',10,'',10,'"B" Coy.'),
