 (19610,19610,'Unknown','WALTER ALAN','PALMER','Able Seaman',25,'South African Naval Forces',831,'H.M.S. Cornwall',2279,'1942-04-06','Killed in action. CORNWALL and her sister ship Dorsetshire were detached from the Fleet when they were sighted by Japanese aircraft. A little later they were overwhelmed by approximately 50 dive-bombers whose accurate attack sank both ships quickly; 190 of Cornwall&#39;s crew of 650 were lost.','No additional information known at this time','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','Panel 74, Column 2.',NULL,'W A','68344','18','Devon',2,'',0,''),
 (19611,19611,'Unknown','WILLIE HENRY DAVID','PALMER','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'4th Regt.',1270,'1917-12-09','Killed in action','Son of Thomas and Sarah Ann Palmer, of 22, Porter St., East London, Cape Province. Also served in German South West Africa. A Railway clerk. Commemoration stone in EAST LONDON (EAST BANK) CEMETERY','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'W H D','14216','30','Somme',15,'',15,'"C" Coy.'),
