 (19778,19778,'Unknown','JOHN PEARSON PARKER','PATTINSON','Gunner',278,'Royal Artillery',643,'102 (The Northumberland Hussars) Lt. A.A./Anti-Tank Regt.',161,'1942-11-01','Killed in action, in the Western Desert','He was born on August 17, 1906, and entered Grey House in February 1920. He passed his Junior Certificate in 1922, and played rugger and cricket for the School 2nd teams, and was a L.-Corporal in the Cadet Corps. After leaving in June 1923, he went mining in the Gwanda district. At the time he joined the Forces he was employed as a mining engineer at Belingwe. He was a keen hockey player, and represented Rhodesia in 1933. On Plumtree School Roll of Honour','Egypt',23,'EL ALAMEIN WAR CEMETERY',775,'30.83819','28.94707 ','XVII. G. 1.',NULL,'J P P','2766228','36','',0,'',0,''),
 (19779,19779,'Unknown','WILLIAM','PATTINSON','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'4th Regt.',1270,'1916-07-11','Died of wounds, when two companies of the 4th S.A.I. relieved elements of the 2nd S.A.I. in Bernafay Wood. A platoon of the 4th S.A.I. was at the Briqueterie and its A Coy, in action in Trone Wood since the 9th, was relieved on the night of the 10th/11th.','Son of William Pattinson, of Florida, Transvaal','France',27,'DIVE COPSE BRITISH CEMETERY, SAILLY-LE-SEC',665,'49.92918','2.60489 ','II. E. 21.',NULL,'W','3528','','Somme',10,'',10,'"B" Coy.'),
