 (20431,20431,'Unknown','CLIFFORD','PLATT','Private',429,'Royal Durban Light Infantry, S.A. Forces',656,'Unknown',0,'1943-12-08','Died as a POW, whilst being loaded onto railway wagons to be transported to Germany, the rail yards at Aquila were bombed by USAF B-25 bombers. About 200 POWs were killed, all who were trapped in locked cattle cars.','Son of James and Alice Platt, of Bulawayo, Southern Rhodesia. Attended Glenwood High School, Durban','Egypt',23,'ALAMEIN MEMORIAL',35,'30.83823','28.94696 ','Column 123.',NULL,'C','6668','32','',0,'',0,''),
 (20432,20432,'Unknown','DENNIS ALFRED','PLATT','Lieutenant',334,'Natal Mounted Rifles, S.A. Forces',504,'1st',515,'1942-10-24','Killed in action, during attack of second battle of Alamein. In the face of heavy machinegun fire the South Africans stormed the strongpoint and after 15 minutes of desperate close quarter fighting they overcame the enemy resistance. The Natal Company [attached to the SA Field Force Battalion], like the FFB before it, suffered heavily - Lt. Dennis PLATT was killed.','Son of Cecil and Hilda Isobel Platt, of Isipingo, Natal. Husband of Pamela Helen (nee Leary) Platt. Born in Isipingo. A Sugar Planter and Miller by profession. Educated at Hilton College, Natal. Commemoration stone in Isipingo Cemetery, Kwazulu Natal.','Egypt',23,'EL ALAMEIN WAR CEMETERY',775,'30.83819','28.94707 ','XI. A. 29.',NULL,'D A','5037','26','',0,'',0,''),
