 (22073,22073,'Unknown','IAN RAMSAY','RUSSELL','Sergeant',492,'South African Infantry',807,'4th Regt.',1270,'1916-10-16','Killed in action, in front the Butte de Warlencourt','Son of John Ramsay Russell and Lene Auld Russell, of 6, Warrender Park Terrace, Edinburgh, Scotland. Native of Wick, Scotland. Born in 1882, and entered George Watson&#39;s College in 1897. On leaving he joined the staff of the Scottish Provident Institution, and shortly afterwards went to Rhodesia. He had been a keen Volunteer, and was Sgt. in one of the Midlothian Artillery Mounted Batteries. He joined the 1st South African Contingent in 1915, and came unscathed through the heavy fighting at Delville Wood.','France',27,'WARLENCOURT BRITISH CEMETERY',2680,'50.08079','2.79911 ','VIII. E. 28.',NULL,'I R','217','34','Pas de Calais',15,'',15,'"C" Coy.'),
 (22074,22074,'Unknown','JOHN','RUSSELL','Flight Lieutenant',253,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'150 Sqdn.',381,'1944-09-17','Killed in action. Took off from Regina to bomb marshalling yards at Bresica near Lake Garda. Aircraft was loaded with 9 SBCs (small bomb containers), each carrying approx. 50 x 4lb incendiary bombs. Plan was to drop high explosive bombs, then incendiary bombs. Squadron Ops Book records: &#39;Not heard of after take off&#39; Squadron Gunnery Leader reported: at 21.00 hours at a height of 5-- feet, heading 13 3 degrees at position 44.55N 11.16, after leaving the target, yellowish white fighter flares were observed on the port beam at about 800yds away. Pilot immediately turned to starboard and resumed course. Shortly afterwards, an explosion in the sky was seen which was suggestive of an aircraft disintegrating and falling to the ground on fire. It burned green and red for a while afterwards','Son of Charles and Elizabeth Russell; husband of Dorothy Isabel Joy Russell (nee Gliddon), of Cape Town, South Africa. He was born on June 10th, 1910, and entered Milner House in January 1929. He passed his Junior Certificate in 1932, was appointed a House Prefect in 1934, lance-corporal in the Cadet Corps; School 2nd Rugger XV, 1934; and he took a keen interest in dramatics, appearing in four school performances. He left Plumtree in December 1934. On Plumtree School Roll of Honour','Italy',39,'PADUA WAR CEMETERY',1910,'45.42165','11.83996 ','VI. A. 12.',NULL,'J','80195','34','',0,'',0,''),
 (22075,22075,'Unknown','JOHN CECIL MATTHEW','RUSSELL','Acting Lance Corporal',30,'Cape Corps',251,'1st',515,'1918-09-20','Killed in action, at Kh Jibeit','Son of Sarah Maria and the late John Russell, of 10 Neil Street, Beaconsfield, Cape Province','Israel',38,'JERUSALEM MEMORIAL',1216,'31.7989','35.24024 ','Panel 57 (Addenda)',NULL,'J C M','147','24','',0,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',0,''),
