 (22849,22849,'Unknown','JOHN ULRIC','SHARLAND','Warrant Officer Class II',595,'South African Corps of Signals',795,'Unknown',0,'1945-07-11','Killed in aircraft accident, 28 (passengers: 24 crew:4) crashed shortly after take off into Lake Victoria, in Douglas C-47 Dakota #6812','Son of William G. Sharland and Gladys S. Sharland, of Sea Point, Cape Town, South Africa. Commemoration stone in Western Cape, Cape Town Central, MAITLAND, Woltemade Cemetery','Kenya',41,'KISUMU CEMETERY',1328,'-0.0828','34.73977 ','Grave 279.',NULL,'J U','73941V','23','',0,'',0,''),
 (22850,22850,'Unknown','ARNOLD','SHARP','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1917-12-08','Killed in action','Son of Mary H. Sharp, of Cape Town','France',27,'FINS NEW BRITISH CEMETERY, SOREL-LE-GRAND',861,'50.0328','3.04904 ','III. A. 15.',NULL,'A','2983','','Somme',1,'',0,''),
 (22851,22851,'Unknown','ARTHUR GRANVILLE','SHARP','Lieutenant',334,'Royal Field Artillery',660,'72nd Army Bde.',1558,'1918-08-23','Killed in action','Son of Arthur Edward and Maud Lilian Sharp. Born in Orange Free State, South Africa.','France',27,'CABARET-ROUGE BRITISH CEMETERY, SOUCHEZ',417,'50.38061','2.7416 ','VIII. N. 8.',NULL,'A G','','20','Pas de Calais',18,'LG Sup 1 Feb 1919 pg 1690 - "On 23rd August, 1918, near Hamelincourt, as forward observation officer, this officer showed conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. Under heavy fire for many hours, he continuously kept touch with the situation, and sent back most valuable reports. At one time he, with two signallers, was isolated between the front line flank companies of two battalions. He succeeded in sending back quickly and accurately the position of that flank, and the gap was filled."',18,'"D" Bty. attd. Guards Div. Artillery.'),
