 (22853,22853,'Unknown','GEORGE GUY','SHARP','Pilot Officer',414,'Royal Air Force',635,'9 Sqdn.',1681,'1941-04-09','Killed in action, Wellington T2473 took-off from RAF Honington at 20.00hrs for an operation on Berlin. Nothing further was heard from this aircraft after take off. The loss of this aircraft was claimed by Orbit. Fritz Gutezeit, Stab III./NJG. The International Red Cross reported later that all the crew had been killed. This was the only aircraft lost from the Squadron on this operation','Son of George W. and Edith Sharp; husband of Jeanette Claire Sharp. B.Sc. Eng. (University of Witwatersrand, South Africa). See more at: http://www.aircrewremembered.com/sharp-george.html#sthash.hJRcxAAS.dpuf','Germany',29,'REICHSWALD FOREST WAR CEMETERY',2124,'51.74094','6.08179 ','14. F. 14.',NULL,'G G','84036','29','Kleve, Nordrhein-Westfalen',1,'',0,''),
 (22854,22854,'Unknown','IAN CHARLES','SHARP','Sapper',469,'South African Signal Service',857,'South African Corps Signal Coy, R.E.',3086,'1917-07-27','Died of spinal meningitis and pericarditis','Son of Charles and Mary Sharp, of 11, Van Der Byl St., Pretoria.','United Kingdom',90,'CAMBRIDGE CITY CEMETERY',434,'52.21324','0.16699 ','C. 3651.',NULL,'I C','12145','17','Cambridgeshire',1,'',0,''),
 (22855,22855,'Unknown','MATTHEW','SHARP','Second Lieutenant',475,'London Regiment',441,'2nd/7th Bn.',951,'1918-02-11','Died of wounds, right thigh and legs','Son of John C. and Janet Sharp, of Rothesay, Bute, Scotland. Usual residence Ladybrand, Orange Free State. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','France',27,'WIMEREUX COMMUNAL CEMETERY',2730,'50.77389','1.61333 ','IV. H. 2.',NULL,'M','','36','Pas de Calais',1,'LG Sup 1 January 1918 pg 45 - "New Years Honours"',0,''),
 (22856,22856,'Unknown','NORMAN EDWARD','SHARP','Flight Lieutenant',253,'Royal Air Force',635,'159 Sqdn.',402,'1942-07-19','Killed in action, Liberator II AL554, on operation to Tobruk, the aircraft may have been damaged over the target area by an enemy fighter. The crew were unable to locate their base of St.Jean and ran out of fuel trying to locate Lydda near Tel Aviv. The aircraft crashed while force landing in Hadera, Palestine. All but one of the eight man crew died','Son of Wallace John and Eva Sharp, of Johannesburg, Transvaal, South Africa. Norman Sharp was granted his commission as Acting P/O on probation on 23rd March 1940, graded as P/O on probation on 13th July 1940 and on 25th September 1940 he was confirmed as P/O. As Acting F/Lt he was awarded the DFC for service with 51 Squadron, Gazetted on 18th July 1941. He had risen to F/O (War Subs) five days previously. He rose to F/Lt (war Subs) on 13th July 1942 having been posted to 159 Squadron prior to this to fly a second Tour. Attended Glenwood High School, Durban.','Israel',38,'KHAYAT BEACH WAR CEMETERY',1288,'32.79922','34.9618 ','A. F. 16.',NULL,'N E','43046','23','',0,'LG 18 July 1941 pg. 4123',0,''),
