 (23847,23847,'Unknown','WILLIAM MAITLAND','SPEKE','Captain',115,'Royal Artillery',643,'72 Field Regt.',1553,'1942-02-18','Killed in action, in Libya','Son of William and Gwendolyn Constance Speke; husband of Joan Margaret Speke, of Ilminster, Somerset. Educated at St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown. At College 1921-22, was in business at Newcastle-on-Tyne in a firm of stockbrokers. Saw service in France and won MC at Dunkirk. Born 14/04/1907.','Libya',45,'KNIGHTSBRIDGE WAR CEMETERY, ACROMA',1344,'32.09586','23.71913 ','11. A. 16.',NULL,'W M','90704','34','',0,'',0,''),
 (23848,23848,'Unknown','ARTHUR','SPENCE','Lance Corporal',311,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Missing in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of Mr. J. Spence, of Beaconsfield Farm, Graaff-Reinet','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'A','6867','23','Somme',6,'',6,'"A" Coy.'),
