 (24671,24671,'Unknown','TRAVERS CHARLES MELVILLE','TABERER','Captain',115,'South African Reserve Mechanical Transport',850,'107 Coy.',180,'1943-07-07','Died of thrombosis, whilst a POW, at POW Camp P.G. 47','Son of Harry and Ethel Taberer; husband of Edith J. Taberer, of Johannesburg, Transaval, South Africa. Educated at St. Andrew&#39;s College, Grahamstown, Eastern Cape and Hilton College, Natal','Italy',39,'MILAN WAR CEMETERY',1672,'45.48499','9.10346 ','V. A. 14.',NULL,'T C M','201496','45','',0,'DSO WW1 with Hampshire Regiment as a 2nd Lt. LG 23 April 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty when in command of his company after the company commander had been killed. Seeing, however, that part of the company detailed for the final objective had become disorganized owing to heavy casualties, on his own initiative he led forward his own company and captured and consolidated the final objective."',0,''),
 (24672,24672,'Unknown','ENOCH','TABETE','Private',429,'Native Military Corps, S.A. Forces',513,'Unknown',0,'1943-03-03','Drowned, on S. S. Nirpura. At 23.22 hours on 3 Mar, 1943, U-160 made her first attack on the convoy DN-21 about 40 miles south of Port St.Johns, South Africa, sank the Harvey W. Scott and Nirpura and damaged the Tibia. 38 men from the Nirpura (Master Thomas George Hodgkinson) were lost and 88 (including the master) were picked up by the SAAF crash launch R-8 and landed at Durban.','Son of Kwaaiman and Maria Tabete; husband of Matapane Tabete, of White River, Transvaal, South Africa.','Myanmar',55,'RANGOON MEMORIAL',2108,'17.03621','96.13212 ','Face 19.',NULL,'E','N/66652','','',0,'',0,''),
