 (25447,25447,'Unknown','ARTHUR PATRICK','TREAMER','Petty Officer',403,'South African Naval Forces',831,'H.M.S.A.S. Parktown',2390,'1942-06-21','Ships were ordered to embark evacuation parties and leave Tobruk harbour when Axis forces started shelling the town on 20 June 1942. Under heavy fire and was the last Allied ship to leave Tobruk. Her decks were cramped with soldiers evacuated from Tobruk and outside the harbour she took a disabled tug in tow. Early the next morning she was attacked at close range by six Italian MAS boats. With her captain killed, nearly half her ship&#39;s company wounded and all ammunition expended, it was finally decided to abandon ship.','Son of Margaret and the late James Treamer, of Wynrush, Briardale Road, Willaston, Wirral, Cheshire, England. Husband of Sonia Annette Treamer, of Inanda, Natal. ***on HMSAS Parktown Memorial in Pieter Roos Park, Parktown.','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','Panel 74, Column 1.',NULL,'A P','71109','41','Devon',1,'',0,''),
 (25448,25448,'Unknown','FRANCIS JOHN','TREANOR','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Killed in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of Francis John Treanor, of 54, North St., Newry, Co. Down, and the late Elizabeth Treanor.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'F J','7725','28','Somme',15,'',15,'"C" Coy.'),
 (25449,25449,'Unknown','TERENCE BRYCE','TREBLE','Sergeant',492,'Royal Air Force Volunteer Reserve',638,'142 Sqdn.',353,'1942-09-09','Killed in action, at 20:23 Wellington IV Z1342 of 142 Sqn coded QT-T took off from Grimsby. The mission was to bomb Frankfurt. On their return, they had problems with the motor and at 04:20 the aircraft ditched into the English Channel. It was a rough ditching. The aircraft broke in two just aft of the main spar and sank in seconds. Taylor had established radio contact with the DF stations and took ditching positions just before the aircraft crashed about 12 miles from the French coast. The entire crew except rear gunner Sgt.T.B.Treble managed to get out of the aircraft and into the dinghy. The latter, who came from South Africa, went down with the tail section.','Son of Clifford John and Ella Dorothy Treble. Native of South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'RUNNYMEDE MEMORIAL',2216,'51.43804','-0.56503 ','Panel 95.',NULL,'T B','778839','19','Surrey',1,'',0,''),
 (25450,25450,'Unknown','JOHN CLARKSON','TREDGOLD','Second Lieutenant',475,'Royal Scots',693,'3rd Bn. attd. 11th Bn.',1100,'1917-04-12','Killed in action','Son of Clarkson Henry Tredgold and Emily Ruth Tredgold, of Cressydale Lydiate, Southern Rhodesia, South Africa.','France',27,'ARRAS MEMORIAL',96,'50.28697','2.76065 ','Bay 1 and 2.',NULL,'J C','','21','Pas de Calais',1,'EG Sup 30 May 1917 pg. 1015 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. He carried out a dangerous reconnaissance and obtained most valuable information. Later, he rescued several wounded men under heavy fire."',0,''),
