 (28052,28052,'Unknown','TOM','WELSH','Captain',115,'South African Medical Corps',817,'1st Field Ambulance',542,'1917-04-12','Died of wounds','Son of James and Jessie Jardine Halliday Welsh. Native of Edinburgh, Scotland. Also served in German South West Africa.','France',27,'ATHIES COMMUNAL CEMETERY EXTENSION',111,'50.30399','2.83734 ','A. 17.',NULL,'T','','33','Pas de Calais',333,'MC LG Sup 10 January 1917 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. He displayed great courage and determination in organising and leading stretcher parties under heavy fire."',333,'attd. 2nd Regt. South African Infantry'),
 (28053,28053,'Unknown','THOMAS HENRY','WELSH','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Killed in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of Thomas Henry and Florence Gertrude Welsh, of Kingston, Wesley, Cape Province.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'T H','8947','26','Somme',19,'',19,'"D" Coy.'),
