 (28168,28168,'Unknown','HENRY GEORGE','WHELAN','Lieutenant',334,'Royal Munster Fusiliers',677,'2nd Bn.',900,'1918-04-11','Died on active service','Son of George and Nelly Whelan, of 15, Hopson Avenue, Davenport Rd., Durban, Natal, South Africa.','Germany',29,'NIEDERZWEHREN CEMETERY',1805,'51.26399','9.46544 ','II. J. 7.',NULL,'H G','','28','Kassel, Hessen',1,'LG Sup 25 August 1916 - "For conspicuous gallantry in action. During a night attack he drove the enemy into their third line of defence, and though wounded early in the attack, hung on to his position till he was ordered to withdraw."',0,''),
 (28169,28169,'Unknown','MAURICE ESMOND','WHELAN','Lieutenant',334,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1918-10-18','Died of wounds, received 17th October','Son of George Whelan, of Circus Place, London Wall, London','France',27,'ROISEL COMMUNAL CEMETERY EXTENSION',2183,'49.95023','3.0984 ','II. B. 20.',NULL,'M E','','','Somme',19,'LG Sup 26 July 1917 pg. 7651 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty in leading his men with the utmost dash and disregard of personal danger during an attack. Though severely wounded by our own barrage, he remained in command until completely exhausted, setting a splendid example to all."',19,'"D" Coy.'),
