 (28576,28576,'Unknown','ANDREW','WILSON','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'1st Regt.',556,'1916-10-18','Missing in action. On the 18th October 1916, at 3.40 am, three companies of the 1st S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The B Coy failed to realize that they had reached Snag Trench and continued beyond it. They had lost touch with their flanks and only some men succeeded to come back. Only a block was established at 50 metres of The Pimple. The D Company launched an attack at 5.45 pm and Snag Trench was occupied but the machine-gun fire from the Butte de Warlencourt took a heavy toll, leaving a small group in the trench.','Son of the late Robert and Elizabeth Wilson.','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'A','7092','25','Somme',6,'',6,'"A" Coy.'),
 (28577,28577,'Unknown','ARTHUR ALEXANDER','WILSON','Second Lieutenant',475,'Argyll and Sutherland Highlanders',172,'3rd Bn.',1097,'1917-04-23','Killed in action','Son of The Very Rev. John Skinner Wilson, Dean of Edinburgh, and Lizzie Mary Wilson (nee Clyne), of Aros, Strathtay, Perthshire. On Keble College Roll of Honour. Entered College in Michaelmas Term, 1911. A member of the 1st Rugby Association XV, 1911 - 1914 (Captain, 1913 - 1914); Seniors&#39; Rugger Trial, 1913. A member of the University Contingent of the Officers&#39; Training Corps. President of the XIII Club, 1914. 3rd Theology and B.A., 1914. Commenced service on 2 June 1916. Second Lieutenant, 3rd Battalion, Princess Louise&#39;s (Argyll and Sutherland Highlanders), 1916; attached to the 2nd Battalion, 1917. Served in France from 1916. ref. South African Roll of Honour 1914-1918','France',27,'HENINEL-CROISILLES ROAD CEMETERY',1123,'50.22793','2.87382 ','II. B. 2.',NULL,'A A','','25','Pas de Calais',1,'',0,''),
