 (28769,28769,'Unknown','HENRY EVELYN','WOOD','Private',429,'South African Infantry',807,'2nd Regt.',925,'1916-10-12','Missing in action. On the 12th October 1916, the 2nd and 4th S.A.I. attacked Snag and Tail Trenches. The assault died away because of machine guns nests, placed well back in prepared positions, which caught the attacking waves at long range.','Friend of Nurse Eileen O&#39;Calloghan, of Henham Hall Hospital, Wangford, Suffolk, England','France',27,'THIEPVAL MEMORIAL',2498,'50.05081','2.68569 ','Pier and Face 4 C.',NULL,'H E','6283','','Somme',15,'',15,'"C" Coy.'),
 (28770,28770,'Unknown','HENRY STEWART','WOOD','Lieutenant',334,'Dorsetshire Regiment',287,'3rd Bn.',1097,'1918-08-11','Killed in action, at Damery Wood','Son of Mrs. Anna Wood, of "Ravenswood," 128, Hatfield St. Gardens, Cape Town, South Africa, and the late Henry Wood. Born at Grahamstown.','France',27,'BOUCHOIR NEW BRITISH CEMETERY',318,'49.74026','2.68846 ','III. A. 19',NULL,'H S','','20','Somme',1,'LG Sup 24 September 1918 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty when in command of a fighting patrol. Coming in contact with a strong enemy patrol he shot the leading man and brought rapid fire to bear on the remainder, who were driven off. Under heavy rifle and machinegun fire he then collected his party and brought them back together with the wounded man whom he had shot. He showed great coolness and able leadership."',0,''),
