 (29967,29970,'Unknown','DANIEL T','DE JAGER','Corporal',166,'South African Air Force',775,'110 Commando Squadron',223,'1989-04-18','Died in Aircraft Accident, Cessna 182 #ZS-FUB. While busy with a 2 day camp, the aircraft struck the ground in the Montague Mountains, east of Robertson. All 4 occupants were killed','Husband of Mrs. E. de Jager, of Melkbos. ***Memorial has D J rather than D T','South Africa',72,'SADF WALL OF REMEMBRANCE, VOORTREKKER MONUMENT, PRETORIA',2234,NULL,NULL,'Cremated - Ashes scattered at the crash site',NULL,'D T','59166538','46','Gauteng',1,'DFC and bar earned in WW2 as Captain/Major #103138V in SAAF. LG Sup 13 July 1943 pg. 3157; Bar to DFC LG Sup 29 December 1944 pg. 5956',179,'Army'),
 (29968,29971,'Unknown','FREDERICK','DE JAGER','Trooper',581,'South African Armoured Corps',779,'61 Mechanised Battalion Group',1442,'1987-10-08','The South African armoured column had penetrated deep into Angola and had just formed a laager to take bearings on their position and objectives. Two Angolan Mig 23 fighter jets had spotted the column, making a low pass initially they turned and came in for the attack. The Migs each dropped 500lbs parachute retarded bombs, one bomb landing just behind the Ratel IFV, literally driving the back axle into the ground and igniting the Ratel&#39;s 90mm ammunition. The second 500lb bomb landing just next to the Ratel. The Ratel&#39;s driver Frikkie de Jager was taking a break and resting in the back of the Ratel when the bombs hit and he died a little later of his injuries caused when the ratel&#39;s ammunition detonated.','Son of Mr. and Mrs. L. T. de Jager','South Africa',72,'RUSTENBURG (DONKERHOEK) CEMETERY',2219,NULL,NULL,'B8 Block 19',NULL,'F','83472712BG','19','North West Province',1,'DFC and bar earned in WW2 as Captain/Major #103138V in SAAF. LG Sup 13 July 1943 pg. 3157; Bar to DFC LG Sup 29 December 1944 pg. 5956',179,'Army'),
