 (30421,30424,'Unknown','GERALD CHRISTIAN','GILDENHUYS','Corporal',166,'South African Technical Services Corps',863,'Western Province Command Workshop',3205,'1981-01-04','Committed suicide, gassed himself, in his car, at Bellville','Husband of Mrs. A. C. Gildenhuys, of Meyerspark, CP.','South Africa',72,'BELLVILLE (STIKLAND) CEMETERY',214,NULL,NULL,'MG. 100.',NULL,'G C','69530152','27','Western Cape',1,'http://www.izkor.gov.il/izkor86.asp?t=98001',634,'attd. Army Battle School'),
 (30422,30425,'Unknown','WILLIAM HENRY BERNARD','GILDENHUYS','Sergeant',492,'South African Infantry Corps',808,'Cape Town Highlanders Regiment',2017,'1977-05-25','Died of wounds, after suffering multiple shrapnel wounds while detached to Alpha Company, 1 Parachute Battalion for special duties. During an anti-insurgent patrol with 1 Parachute Battalion to the rear of the Cape Town Highlander&#39;s position and north-east of Ondangwa where mining of vehicle tracks had become a common feature, he was killed during an extremely gallant attempt to save others when saw the safety pin being accidentally extracted from a soldiers rifle-grenade by a thorn bush. He rushed forward, grabbed the rifle and tried to fire off the grenade to prevent injuries to the other soldiers in the patrol but the rifle grenade exploded before he could do this. Rifleman F.E. Eksteen, W.R. Hamber, R.J. Schonken and A.J. Rohlandt were all injured in the explosion. Sergeant Gildenhuys was posthumously awarded the Honoris Crux for his gallant action that ultimately cost him his life','No additional information known at this time','South Africa',72,'CAPE TOWN (MAITLAND) CEMETERY',452,'-33.91592','18.52805 ','MG. 100.',NULL,'W H B','65778532BV','28','Western Cape',1,'http://www.izkor.gov.il/izkor86.asp?t=98001',634,'attd. Army Battle School'),
 (30423,30426,'Unknown','ALAN','GINGLES','Lieutenant',334,'5 Reconnaissance Regiment',1002,'Cape Town Highlanders Regiment',2017,'1981-10-15','Killed in action, against terrorists in the operational area. Killed whilst leading a small Special Forces team carrying out an operation against the Harare-Beira railway near Doeroi, in Mozambique, some 300 miles from the South African border. It would appear there was a premature detonation whilst they were laying their demolition charges, and Alan and three of his comrades were killed. FRELIMO found their bodies and equipment, but as per SOPs, nothing was being carried which would enable them to identify the men.','From Larne, Co. Antrim, Northern Ireland. Having graduated from the Royal Military Academy Sandhurst, he was commissioned into the Royal Irish Rangers, and served with the Second Battalion in Hemer, West Germany. Thirsting for adventure, and an opportunity to fight terrorism, Alan left the British Army and went to Rhodesia, where he passed selection and became a member of the Selous Scouts. Following the demise of Rhodesia, he joined the SADF. At the time of his death, he was serving with the 5th Reconnaissance Regiment at Phalaborwa, in the Eastern Transvaal. Alan was not forgotten by his brother officers of 2 Royal Irish. They commissioned a portrait of him, which was hung in the Officers&#39; Mess','South Africa',72,'SADF WALL OF REMEMBRANCE, VOORTREKKER MONUMENT, PRETORIA',2234,NULL,NULL,'Body not recovered',NULL,'A','65778532BV','28','Gauteng',1,'http://www.izkor.gov.il/izkor86.asp?t=98001',634,'attd. Army Battle School'),
