 (30618,30621,'Unknown','SAREL PETRUS','HANNEMAN','Corporal',166,'NORTHER',537,'8 South African Infantry Battalion',1628,'1979-09-18','Died in Military Vehicle Accident, at Potgietersrus','Son of Mrs. M. E. S. Prinsloo','South Africa',72,'SPRINGS (GEDULD) CEMETERY',2334,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'S P','76334911BG','19','Gauteng',1,'LG Sup 7 June 1951 pg. 3097 - "King&#39;s birthday"',22,'"E" Coy.'),
 (30619,30622,'Unknown','STEPHEN SOLOMAN','HANSEN','Second Lieutenant',475,'South African Infantry Corps',808,'6 South African Infantry Battalion',1421,'1981-05-20','Killed in action, in Sector 10, landmine. Lt Steven Hansen and a section of engineers were busy with landmine sweeping in the Odila area. They had found tracks and then traced these tracks backwards to sweep any landmines planted in these tracks. Swapo had set up a cheesemine bound with cortex to 3 British Mk7 mines and this was set off by their buffel.','Son of Mr. and Mrs. S. S. Hansen. Whilst on a vehicle patrol from Okankolo north-east along the Odilla river bed, the Buffel that Lt Hansen was in hit two landmines. The two mines under the vehicle were initiated by one in the track. A hole was punched directly through the base of the vehicle which then caught fire from the fuel tanks. The resulting explosion killed Lt Hansen.','South Africa',72,'PORT ELIZABETH (FOREST HILL) CEMETERY',2012,NULL,NULL,'Section 28, Row D, Number 7',NULL,'S S','77496321BG','19','Eastern Cape',1,'LG Sup 7 June 1951 pg. 3097 - "King&#39;s birthday"',22,'"E" Coy.'),
 (30620,30623,'Unknown','IAN STEWARD','HANSLO','Private',429,'South African Corps of Engineers',793,'6 South African Infantry Battalion',1421,'1989-02-07','Died in Military Vehicle Accident, at Kroonstad, on guard duty at gate of Engineer School when hit by 10 ton truck','Son of Mr. P. D. Hanslo','South Africa',72,'SADF WALL OF REMEMBRANCE, VOORTREKKER MONUMENT, PRETORIA',2234,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'I S','85356046BG','19','Gauteng',1,'LG Sup 7 June 1951 pg. 3097 - "King&#39;s birthday"',22,'"E" Coy.'),
 (30621,30624,'Unknown','RICHARD ARTHUR','HARBURN','Captain',115,'South African Air Force',775,'2 Sqdn.',657,'1952-02-11','Killed in action, in North American F-51 Mustang #375. A four aircraft &#39;C&#39; Flight formation, led by Lt Baransky, were en route to their assigned target at CT062459. After climbing through cloud to 1000 feet, Capt R.A. Harburn, flying in the No.2 position, reported that his aircraft was leaking something. On checking, Lt Baransky discovered that the starboard side of Capt Harburn&#39;s aircraft was covered in oil, and he ordered the latter to return to base at K-46. He then ordered the remaining two aircraft to join with &#39;B&#39; Flight, as he would escort Capt Harburn back to base. The two pilots then requested permission to drop their bombs, and were instructed to do so either north or south of K-46. When five miles from K-46, Lt Baransky ordered Capt Harburn to drop his bombs in the nearby river, which Capt Harburn proceeded to do. He then called Lt Branasky to ask if the latter had seen his bombs drop, and received a negative response. Lt Baransky told Capt Harburn to fly past the airfield Control Tower for an inspection, and shortly after this the Control Tower reported that Capt Harburn&#39;s Mustang had crashed into the ground and burst into flames. It appears that he did attempt to bale out, but from marks found on his aircraft&#39;s cockpit canopy, he may have been struck in the head by the canopy as it was released, and this rendered him unconscious.','Son of Mr. A. G. Harburn, of Ifafa Beach, Natal. ref. 85 years of SAAF','South Korea',73,'UNITED NATIONS PUSAN CEMETERY',2570,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'R A','P1/28648','27','Gauteng',1,'LG Sup 7 June 1951 pg. 3097 - "King&#39;s birthday"',22,'"E" Coy.'),
