 (31294,31297,'Unknown','JACK NORMAN','LELLYET','Second Lieutenant',475,'South African Air Force',775,'2 Sqdn.',657,'1952-02-29','Killed in action, in North American F-51 Mustang #362. Capt Leathers, with 2Lt J.N. Lellyet as his No.2, was leading four aircraft on a training flight, a simulated interdiction strike on a rail bridge over a river. They arrived over the designated area at 10500 feet, and commenced their attacks in echelon port formation. Shortly after pulling out of his dive at 3000 feet, Capt Leathers looked back and saw an explosion short of the target and a half mile to starboard. The No.3 then reported over the radio that "No.2 has gone in.". No.3 and No.4 aircraft proceeded to orbit the crash site while Capt Leathers flew back to K-10 to report the accident, as he was unable to radio them. Lt Lellyet&#39;s body was recovered.','ref. 85 years of SAAF','South Korea',73,'UNITED NATIONS PUSAN CEMETERY',2570,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'J N','P1/40756','22','Kwazulu Natal',1,'http://www.izkor.gov.il/izkor82.asp?t=92341',179,'Army'),
 (31295,31298,'Unknown','STEVEN ERIC','LELONG','Sapper',469,'1 Parachute Battalion',7,'Combat Engineering Unit',2050,'1988-02-22','Died in a friendly fire incident in Sector 10. A team of combat engineers were mistakenly ambushed by 102 Bn Reaction Force. Spr Lelong died from 60mm mortar shrapnel wounds.','Son of Mr. and Mrs. E. M. R. Lelong, of Queensburgh.','South Africa',72,'QUEENSBURGH CEMETERY',2083,NULL,NULL,'Cremation Niche',NULL,'S E','84506179BG','19','Kwazulu Natal',1,'http://www.izkor.gov.il/izkor82.asp?t=92341',179,'Army'),
