 (32426,32429,'Unknown','LAURENCE WALTER','PARSONSON','Lieutenant',334,'South African Air Force',775,'2 Sqdn.',657,'1952-01-06','Killed in action, in North American F-51 Mustang #353. Lt L.W. Parsonson was briefed to lead "A" Flight on an interdiction mission against a railroad yard at YC4394. En route his No.4 reported that he had engine problems, so Lt Parsonson ordered him to return to base, escorted by the No.3 aircraft. He then requested permission for his two remaining aircraft to join another flight, led by Major Lyon, who were en route to the same target. This was granted. All six aircraft attacked their primary target with bombs, and proceeded to their secondary target north east of Kumsong. This they attacked with rockets and machine guns. Lt Parsonson, flying in the No.5 position, reported that he had been hit, and was instructed to fly south. However, before he could reach friendly lines he reported that his aircraft was on fire and shortly after that he crashed. The flight searched the area for his parachute or any sign of life without success.','Son of Bertha Parsonson, of Seymour, Cape Province. Husband of Ambrosia Merle Parsonson, of Bloemfontein. ref. 85 years of SAAF','South Korea',73,'UNITED NATIONS PUSAN MEMORIAL',2571,NULL,NULL,'Panel 13',NULL,'L W','P1/35870','30','Gauteng',1,'LG Sup 3 March 1917 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. He has at all times set a splendid example of courage and determination"',118,'Air Force'),
 (32427,32430,'Unknown','STEPHEN TREVOR','PARVESS','Private',429,'South African Ordnance Services Corps',835,'1 Maintenance Unit',94,'1977-01-21','Died during his basic training for National Service. His death was caused by an inherited medical condition that resulted in his body over heating during a training exercise His vital organs could not cope with the continuous high body temperatures brought on by the inability of his body to switch from heating to cooling mode. Despite the efforts of the medical team he passed away and is greatly missed as he was a lovely person','Brother of Vaughan Parvess','South Africa',72,'SADF WALL OF REMEMBRANCE, VOORTREKKER MONUMENT, PRETORIA',2234,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'S T','75342402BA','18','Gauteng',1,'LG Sup 3 March 1917 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. He has at all times set a splendid example of courage and determination"',118,'Air Force'),
 (32428,32431,'Unknown','STEPHEN TREVOR','PASSEN','Second Lieutenant',475,'SA Army',704,'1 Maintenance Unit',94,'1993-01-01','Died during his basic training for National Service. His death was caused by an inherited medical condition that resulted in his body over heating during a training exercise His vital organs could not cope with the continuous high body temperatures brought on by the inability of his body to switch from heating to cooling mode. Despite the efforts of the medical team he passed away and is greatly missed as he was a lovely person','Brother of Vaughan Parvess','South Africa',72,'SADF WALL OF REMEMBRANCE, VOORTREKKER MONUMENT, PRETORIA',2234,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'H','75342402BA','18','Gauteng',179,'LG Sup 3 March 1917 - "For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. He has at all times set a splendid example of courage and determination"',179,'Army'),
