 (33058,33062,'Unknown','ROBERT LESLIE','STAATS','Second Lieutenant',475,'South African Air Force',775,'2 Sqdn.',657,'1952-01-17','Killed in action, in North American F-51 Mustang #374. 2Lt R.L. Staats had just recently returned for a second tour of operations, and was authorised to test Mustang 374, which had just undergone a maintenance check. He was seen circling K-10 airfield but when he failed to land after half an hour, attempts were made to contact him from the Control Tower, but he failed to respond. A message was almost immediately received that a Mustang had crashed into Chinhae Bay. A search failed to find any trace of him or his aircraft, and only three days later was the aircraft eventually found. South Korean divers recovered 2Lt Staats&#39; body.','ref. 85 years of SAAF','South Korea',73,'UNITED NATIONS PUSAN CEMETERY',2570,NULL,NULL,'Alternative Commemoration until grave found',NULL,'R L','P1/40016','23','Kwazulu Natal',1,'Unknown',179,'Army'),
 (33059,33063,'Unknown','CLIFTON','STACEY','Sergeant',492,'South African Air Force',775,'87 Helicopter Flying School',1655,'1981-08-25','Killed in action, hit by an RPG a short while before Operation Protea in Angola, in Allouette III #48(2)','Husband of Mrs. H. C. Stacey. ref. 85 years of SAAF','South Africa',72,'RUSTENBURG (DONKERHOEK) CEMETERY',2219,NULL,NULL,'A14 Block 19',NULL,'C','75540740PE','21','North West Province',1,'Unknown',179,'Army'),
 (33060,33064,'Unknown','HERBERT MUNRO','STAINBANK','Civilian',137,'Magistrate of Mahlabatini',460,'87 Helicopter Flying School',1655,'1906-05-03','Murdered, shot by Cakijana, on the south bank of the White Mfolozi River whilst collecting taxes','Husband of Clarise Muriel (nee Winter) Stainbank. Born 1872 Durban. He attended Durban High School before becoming a government clerk. During the Boer war, Stainbank enlisted in the Colonial Scouts until March 1900. He was one of a select 30 who were chosen as Warrenâ€™s Bodyguard. After this, he joined the Nottingham Rifle Association and participated in camps that would deter a possible second invasion into Natal. Mr. Stainbank, a member of a prominent Natal family, was camped there with his wife and child, and members of the NP. Local chiefs expressed horror at the murder, giving an indication that an attempt to indict the tribesmen of the area to revolt had been unsuccessful','South Africa',72,'MAHLABATINI AREA CEMETERY ?',1568,NULL,NULL,'A14 Block 19',NULL,'H M','75540740PE','34','Kwazulu Natal',1,'Unknown',179,'Army'),
