 (33791,33795,'Unknown','FREDERICK GERHARDUS','WANNENBURG','Sergeant',492,'1 Reconnaissance Regiment',9,'32 (Buffalo) Battalion',1013,'1975-12-07','Died of wounds, received during Operation Quipico, a forerunner to the battle at Bridge 14, he was part of a 10 man Recce team, landed by helicopter. They advanced in single file with Botes leading,  Wannenberg second in the line, followed by Capt Venter. As they neared a road, Capt Venter moved forward and told Wannenberg to stop, who, in turn, told Botes. At that moment they were caught in heavy fire (ambush) and Wannenberg was hit several times and Capt Venter was wounded in the hand. They were unable to raise Cela by radio and so walked back to a river nearby. Only the following morning were they able to summon a chopper but Wannenberg had already died of his wounds.','Son of Mr. F. G. Wannenburg','South Africa',72,'RUSTENBURG (DONKERHOEK) CEMETERY',2219,NULL,NULL,'OLD SEC 231',NULL,'F G','67437996PE','24','North West Province',1,'He distinguished himself as part of Bravo Group during Op Savannah',179,'Army'),
 (33792,33796,'Unknown','DAVID JOHN','WARD','Staff Sergeant',531,'South African Infantry Corps',808,'101 Battalion',155,'1984-03-11','Killed in action, contact with enemy. He was killed in an ambush when he was wounded in the right hip but died of blood loss','Son of Mr. and Mrs. J. A. S. Ward, of Johannesburg. His ashes lie next to his grandmother&#39;s in the St. John the Baptist Anglican Church.','South Africa',72,'HARRISMITH CEMETERY',1078,'-28.269','29.13346 ','Ashes at St. John The Baptist Anglican Church, Harrismith, Free State',NULL,'D J','75289629PE','24','Free State',1,'He distinguished himself as part of Bravo Group during Op Savannah',179,'Army'),
