 (41203,41220,'Unknown','FREDERICK HERBERT','COOMBS','Stoker 1st Class',539,'Royal Navy',685,'H.M.S. "Indefatigable."',2239,'1916-05-31','This was the first of the three British battlecruisers to be sunk at Jutland. She was struck by at least two salvoes from Hipper&#39;s battlecruisers around 15.58 on 31st May 1916. HMS Indefatigable was seen to sink by the stern, rolling over to port. There were two survivors from a crew of 1, 019.','Son of Lewis Coombs, of Clifford Farm, Bath, Somerset. Born Cape Town, South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','15.',NULL,'F H','K/17342','23','Devon',1,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',10,'"B" Coy.'),
 (41204,41221,'Unknown','CHARLES CECIL','DANIELS','Stoker 1st Class',539,'Royal Navy',685,'H.M.S. "Indefatigable."',2239,'1916-08-15','HMS E41 collided with E4 on the surface during exercises off Harwick on 15 August 1916. Sixteen crewmembers were lost, but fifteen escaped including seven from the bottom. Six crew members trapped in the submarine as she sank were able to escape by waiting under the conning-tower until the air pressure building up as the submarine sank blew the hatch open and allowed them to float to the surface. Chief Petty Officer William Brown was left behind, but was eventually able to escape from the engine room after an hour and a half in the sunken submarine.','Brother of Fred Daniels, of 69, Caledonian Rd, Kings Cross, N. Born Cape Town, South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'SHOTLEY (ST. MARY) CHURCHYARD',2292,'51.97747','1.25586 ','R.N. Plot. 100.',NULL,'C C','K/22606','21','Suffolk',1,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',10,'"B" Coy.'),
 (41205,41222,'Unknown','JAMES','MICHAUX','Able Seaman',25,'Royal Navy',685,'H.M.S. "Indefatigable."',2239,'1916-05-31','This was the first of the three British battlecruisers to be sunk at Jutland. She was struck by at least two salvoes from Hipper&#39;s battlecruisers around 15.58 on 31st May 1916. HMS Indefatigable was seen to sink by the stern, rolling over to port. There were two survivors from a crew of 1, 019.','Adopted son of Rachel J. Walker, of 25, Argyle St., Oxford. Born Cape Town, South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'PLYMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',1989,'50.36573','-4.14223 ','12.',NULL,'J','J/23521','18','Devon',1,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',10,'"B" Coy.'),
 (41206,41223,'Unknown','REGINALD','MOORE','Able Seaman',25,'Royal Navy',685,'H.M.S. "Indefatigable."',2239,'1917-02-21','Killed in action, hit a mine laid by the German submarine UC 23 while on a voyage from Styros Island to Mudros while in government service','Son of Annie Moore, of 1, Woodland Place, Rohais Rd., Guernsey. Born Pietermaritzburg, South Africa','United Kingdom',90,'PORTSMOUTH NAVAL MEMORIAL',2031,'50.78242','-1.09599 ','25.',NULL,'R','J/41044','24','Hampshire',1,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',10,'"B" Coy.'),
 (41207,41224,'Unknown','HARRY WALTER','MOSS','Ordinary Seaman',389,'Royal Navy',685,'H.M.S. "Indefatigable."',2239,'1918-04-14','Died of cerebral spinal meningitis','Son of Walter and Sarah Jane Moss, of 13, Disraeli St., Dewsbury Rd., Leeds. Born Cape Town, South Africa.','United Kingdom',90,'HASLAR ROYAL NAVAL CEMETERY',1089,'50.78325','-1.13732 ','E. 31. 9.',NULL,'H W','J/84318','18','Hampshire',1,'***Accepted by CWGC for War Grave Status & burial site under investigation',10,'"B" Coy.'),
